1
00:00:01,233 --> 00:00:04,267
♪ In the not too distant
Future ♪

2
00:00:04,367 --> 00:00:06,333
♪ Next Sunday AD ♪

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:10,367
♪ There was a guy named Joel ♪

4
00:00:10,467 --> 00:00:13,467
♪ Not too different
From you or me ♪

5
00:00:13,567 --> 00:00:16,500
♪ He worked at
Gizmonic Institute ♪

6
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:19,767
♪ Just another face
In a red jumpsuit ♪

7
00:00:19,867 --> 00:00:22,600
♪ He did a good job
Cleaning up the place ♪

8
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:24,133
♪ But his bosses
Didn't like him ♪

9
00:00:24,233 --> 00:00:26,833
♪ So they shot him into space ♪

10
00:00:29,033 --> 00:00:31,967
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies ♪

11
00:00:32,067 --> 00:00:35,033
♪ The worst we can find
La la la ♪

12
00:00:35,133 --> 00:00:38,100
♪ He'll have to sit
And watch them all ♪

13
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:41,167
♪ And we'll monitor his mind
La la la ♪

14
00:00:41,267 --> 00:00:44,200
♪ Now keep in mind
Joel can't control ♪

15
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:47,267
♪ Where the movies begin or end
La-la-la ♪

16
00:00:47,367 --> 00:00:50,267
♪ Because he used those
Special parts ♪

17
00:00:50,367 --> 00:00:53,833
♪ To make his robot friends ♪

18
00:00:55,467 --> 00:00:57,400
♪ Robot roll call ♪
♪ Cambot! ♪

19
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:58,933
- ♪ Pan left ♪
- ♪ Gypsy! ♪

20
00:00:59,033 --> 00:01:00,367
- ♪ Hi girl! ♪
- ♪ Tom Servo! ♪

21
00:01:00,467 --> 00:01:01,433
- ♪ What a cool guy ♪

22
00:01:01,533 --> 00:01:02,900
- ♪ Croooow! ♪
- ♪ Wisecracker ♪

23
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,833
♪ If you're wondering
How he eats and breathes ♪

24
00:01:05,933 --> 00:01:08,867
♪ And other science facts
La la la ♪

25
00:01:08,967 --> 00:01:10,133
♪ Then repeat to yourself ♪

26
00:01:10,233 --> 00:01:15,033
♪ "It's just a show
I should really just relax" ♪

27
00:01:15,133 --> 00:01:20,833
♪ For Mystery
Science Theater 3000 ♪

28
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:26,533
- Oh Larry, I brought you
a little something

29
00:01:26,633 --> 00:01:29,233
from the mad scientist
convention this year.

30
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:31,400
Little gift,
little tchotchke for you.

31
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:33,267
- Oh, what is it, you?

32
00:01:33,367 --> 00:01:35,400
- It's the Isaac Asimov
body splash.

33
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:38,433
It's part of the foundation
trilogy gift set.

34
00:01:38,533 --> 00:01:39,967
- Oh, and it's pretty too.

35
00:01:40,067 --> 00:01:41,700
It smells like space!

36
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:44,500
- Yes, and it also goes
with the mad scientist

37
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:45,867
masculine hygiene...

38
00:01:45,967 --> 00:01:48,500
- Oh, let me try that!
- Later, later.

39
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:50,533
It's time to get Joel
on the old horn here,

40
00:01:50,633 --> 00:01:52,367
give him our experiment
this week.

41
00:01:52,467 --> 00:01:53,833
Would you like
to do the honors?

42
00:01:53,933 --> 00:01:55,733
- Oh, you are
something special.

43
00:01:55,833 --> 00:01:56,733
[GASPS]

44
00:01:56,833 --> 00:01:57,867
[GRUNTS]

45
00:01:57,967 --> 00:02:02,233
Come in, Joel,
you tree rotating skanky boy.

46
00:02:02,333 --> 00:02:04,233
- Not bad, not bad.

47
00:02:04,333 --> 00:02:05,300
- Hey, sirs.

48
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:07,200
Hey, Cambot,
bring it in a little bit.

49
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:09,567
I'm ready for this week's
invention exchange.

50
00:02:09,667 --> 00:02:11,733
This is called
the Cairo-Gyro

51
00:02:11,833 --> 00:02:14,833
and if you happen to suffer
from back, neck, or pelvis pain,

52
00:02:14,933 --> 00:02:16,600
you strap it on
and let it go.

53
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:18,867
It does the work for you,
watch.

54
00:02:36,733 --> 00:02:38,067
What do you think?

55
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:41,467
- Mmm.

56
00:02:41,567 --> 00:02:43,367
I'd like to strap
that on you, Larry.

57
00:02:43,467 --> 00:02:46,067
- Yeah, that'd be
a nice way to unwind.

58
00:02:46,167 --> 00:02:47,067
[GIGGLES]

59
00:02:47,167 --> 00:02:49,367
- Oh, stop it.
- I'm sorry.

60
00:02:49,467 --> 00:02:51,700
- Here's our invention
this week, Joelette.

61
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:55,033
As you know, the old
squirting joke flower

62
00:02:55,133 --> 00:02:58,500
has lost its ability
to shock or surprise.

63
00:02:58,600 --> 00:02:59,933
- We souped it up though.

64
00:03:00,033 --> 00:03:01,767
We came up with
the burning boutonniere

65
00:03:01,867 --> 00:03:03,933
featuring the flame-flower.

66
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:06,200
- I'd like to see anyone

67
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:09,267
that isn't surprised
by that, Joelenne.

68
00:03:09,367 --> 00:03:10,700
Yes.

69
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:12,467
[LAUGHING]

70
00:03:12,567 --> 00:03:16,933
- I cannot believe you guys.
That is so hateful.

71
00:03:17,033 --> 00:03:19,600
BOTH:
Thank you!

72
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:21,900
- Well, our burnt offering
this week, Joel,

73
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,467
is a little scientific nugget
called The Corpse Vanishes.

74
00:03:25,567 --> 00:03:28,967
- It features Bela Lugosi
stewing in his own mediocrity.

75
00:03:29,067 --> 00:03:32,033
- Yes, but first another one
of those space stinkers,

76
00:03:32,133 --> 00:03:35,167
Commando Cody
and the Bridge of Death.

77
00:03:35,267 --> 00:03:36,433
- Enjoy.

78
00:03:36,533 --> 00:03:37,433
[BUZZER]

79
00:03:37,533 --> 00:03:39,633
- Movie sign!

80
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,000
[♪♪♪]

81
00:03:55,467 --> 00:03:57,067
TOM:
Another republic cereal?

82
00:03:57,167 --> 00:03:58,700
JOEL:
Yeah, used to my head-cranker.

83
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:00,400
I showed it to the professors.

84
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:01,467
CROW:
How'd they like it?

85
00:04:01,567 --> 00:04:03,833
JOEL:
I think they liked it.

86
00:04:03,933 --> 00:04:05,300
And they had this
flame-flower thing,

87
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:06,400
it was very weird.

88
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:09,200
TOM:
Radar men from the moon!

89
00:04:11,367 --> 00:04:13,033
They liked
the head-cranker, huh?

90
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:16,433
George Wallace,
Aline Towne, Roy Barcroft,

91
00:04:16,533 --> 00:04:20,133
William Bakewell,
Clayton Moore.

92
00:04:20,233 --> 00:04:22,833
With Peter Brocco,
Bob Stevenson, Don Walters,

93
00:04:22,933 --> 00:04:25,800
Tom Steele, Dale Van Sickel,
Wilson Wood, Noel Cravat,

94
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:28,900
Baynes Barron,
Paul McGuire, Ted Thorpe,

95
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,233
♪ And written by
Ronald Davidson ♪

96
00:04:33,300 --> 00:04:34,800
♪ John MacBurnie, Fred A... ♪

97
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:36,000
JOEL:
Okay, that's good. That's fine.

98
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:38,000
CROW:
Make him read the small print.

99
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:39,900
JOEL:
Yeah, what's that symbol
mean over there?

100
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,800
I always wondered about it.
TOM: It means Golden Club.

101
00:04:42,900 --> 00:04:44,700
JOEL:
Golden Club turkey?

102
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:46,633
TOM: Uh, yes.
JOEL: Okay.

103
00:04:46,733 --> 00:04:49,900
TOM:
This is in fact a Golden Club
turkey of a movie.

104
00:04:52,367 --> 00:04:54,600
Chapter Three,
Bridge of Death.

105
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:56,500
CROW:
I think it's about dentistry.

106
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:57,600
JOEL:
Yeah.

107
00:05:00,033 --> 00:05:01,267
CROW:
Hey, how come Ted's so big

108
00:05:01,367 --> 00:05:03,233
and Commando Cody
is so small?

109
00:05:03,333 --> 00:05:05,100
JOEL:
Oh Crow, that's a common
movie device

110
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:06,400
called foreshortening.

111
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:07,400
CROW:
Oh.

112
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:08,467
JOEL:
Yeah, they use it all the time.

113
00:05:08,567 --> 00:05:09,867
CROW:
Instead of butter.

114
00:05:16,833 --> 00:05:17,733
TOM:
That's like someone's

115
00:05:17,833 --> 00:05:19,600
throwing their [indistinct] up.

116
00:05:19,700 --> 00:05:21,800
JOEL:
It's a Whamo Magic Window.

117
00:05:26,567 --> 00:05:27,467
TOM:
Evil [indistinct] men

118
00:05:27,567 --> 00:05:29,133
continued to use
the constant heat ray,

119
00:05:29,233 --> 00:05:31,100
which sounded like
a Chevy horn.

120
00:05:34,667 --> 00:05:36,467
CROW:
Hey, surf's up!

121
00:05:40,267 --> 00:05:42,567
- Whoa, maybe we can
get out along that side.

122
00:05:42,667 --> 00:05:44,033
- What about that tank?

123
00:05:44,133 --> 00:05:45,267
- We may be able
to take care of it.

124
00:05:45,367 --> 00:05:47,033
JOEL:
What about the camera crew?

125
00:05:51,267 --> 00:05:52,167
TOM:
They were annihilated,

126
00:05:52,267 --> 00:05:55,133
the entire crew
was annihilated.

127
00:05:55,233 --> 00:05:56,267
But they got the shot.

128
00:05:56,367 --> 00:05:59,167
JOEL:
Flash him,
he's got his high beams on.

129
00:05:59,267 --> 00:06:00,533
CROW:
The blood stream.

130
00:06:01,933 --> 00:06:03,633
- Let's try this one.

131
00:06:03,733 --> 00:06:05,167
CROW:
Quick, up this hill.

132
00:06:06,533 --> 00:06:08,967
Try not to look like
Mr. Peanut.

133
00:06:09,067 --> 00:06:10,667
JOEL:
Or Mr. Salty.

134
00:06:13,933 --> 00:06:15,733
CROW:
Or Mr. Drip.

135
00:06:15,833 --> 00:06:17,133
TOM:
Or Mr. Bubble.

136
00:06:19,033 --> 00:06:20,733
- Give me a hand grenade.

137
00:06:20,833 --> 00:06:21,833
TOM:
Hand grenade?

138
00:06:23,167 --> 00:06:24,600
CROW:
Hand grenade.

139
00:06:24,700 --> 00:06:26,800
TOM:
Eat shrapnel tire, boys.

140
00:06:29,333 --> 00:06:31,567
JOEL:
Boy, that really hurt 'em.

141
00:06:31,667 --> 00:06:32,667
- He must've gotten off.

142
00:06:32,767 --> 00:06:33,667
- Let's move out of here.

143
00:06:33,767 --> 00:06:35,967
CROW:
What a couple of retreads.

144
00:06:36,067 --> 00:06:38,567
Remember, signal your intent,
use your mirrors,

145
00:06:38,667 --> 00:06:42,167
put it in reverse,
mind your space cushion.

146
00:06:42,267 --> 00:06:43,900
TOM:
Space cushion?

147
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:45,600
CROW:
It's what they're sitting on.

148
00:06:47,367 --> 00:06:48,400
- That does it, Ted.

149
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:50,067
Let's get back
to the rocket ship.

150
00:06:51,267 --> 00:06:53,600
TOM: Okay.
JOEL: Don't bang into anything.

151
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:57,267
TOM:
Meanwhile...

152
00:06:57,367 --> 00:06:59,033
- Too bad
you lost that ray gun.

153
00:06:59,133 --> 00:07:01,600
JOEL:
Jim never drinks coffee
on our home planet.

154
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:02,600
- ...of getting one.

155
00:07:02,700 --> 00:07:03,800
We couldn't possibly
attack their city

156
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:05,067
with any of our weapons.

157
00:07:05,167 --> 00:07:06,500
- That isn't the worst of it.

158
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:08,300
They've already tracked us
halfway to the ship,

159
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:09,833
so they know about
where we are.

160
00:07:09,933 --> 00:07:12,000
CROW:
Spacemen just don't look
at home in a breakfast nook.

161
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:13,133
- One blast from that ray gun

162
00:07:13,233 --> 00:07:15,233
will pop this ship
like a toy balloon.

163
00:07:15,333 --> 00:07:17,000
- Maybe we'd better
get back to earth.

164
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:19,067
JOEL:
What do you think, mmm?

165
00:07:19,167 --> 00:07:20,433
- I think so.

166
00:07:20,533 --> 00:07:23,167
We've accomplished the most
important part of our mission.

167
00:07:23,267 --> 00:07:24,233
We discovered
that Redick

168
00:07:24,333 --> 00:07:25,900
is planning to lead
an invasion from the moon

169
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,933
as soon as the saboteurs
finish opening up our defenses.

170
00:07:29,033 --> 00:07:31,500
And it's up to us to get
the information back to earth.

171
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:33,067
How soon can we take off?

172
00:07:33,167 --> 00:07:34,167
- Well, I've gotta
build up the pressure

173
00:07:34,267 --> 00:07:35,967
in all the tanks
and warm up the jets.

174
00:07:36,067 --> 00:07:38,667
Maybe a couple of hours.
- Then get started.

175
00:07:38,767 --> 00:07:41,033
I'll fly out and scout around
until you're ready.

176
00:07:41,133 --> 00:07:42,500
CROW:
Oh, and the woman
will clean up.

177
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:45,067
How progressive.

178
00:07:45,167 --> 00:07:48,067
[TOM HUMMING]

179
00:07:51,433 --> 00:07:52,433
Uh?

180
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:55,033
Mmm.

181
00:07:55,133 --> 00:07:56,433
Uh?

182
00:07:56,533 --> 00:07:57,800
That's not one of ours.

183
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:00,133
Hmm, I'd better-- hmm.

184
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:04,767
CROW:
Hey Joel,
what's that he's cranking?

185
00:08:04,867 --> 00:08:06,633
JOEL:
I think it's
some kind of radio.

186
00:08:06,733 --> 00:08:09,167
TOM:
It's his crotch mounted
cordless phone.

187
00:08:10,567 --> 00:08:12,700
JOEL:
That guy's speaking
into a cruller.

188
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:14,100
CROW:
Maybe he's Danish.

189
00:08:14,200 --> 00:08:16,367
TOM:
If it's the moon,
it's a cheese Danish.

190
00:08:16,467 --> 00:08:18,433
- ...our scout car
was attacked.

191
00:08:18,533 --> 00:08:19,533
- Splendid.

192
00:08:19,633 --> 00:08:21,967
I'll send another car out
after it at once.

193
00:08:22,067 --> 00:08:23,433
Stay there and keep watch.

194
00:08:23,533 --> 00:08:24,533
- Yes, sir.

195
00:08:28,267 --> 00:08:31,867
JOEL:
Hiiiiiiii!

196
00:08:31,967 --> 00:08:35,067
Oh, my shoes are untied.

197
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:39,233
CROW:
He's wearing
hand thrown pottery.

198
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:43,533
My suit's more comfortable

199
00:08:43,633 --> 00:08:46,133
and I'm willing
to fight about it.

200
00:08:46,233 --> 00:08:47,133
TOM:
Our own Commando Cody

201
00:08:47,233 --> 00:08:48,500
is wearing a stylish frock

202
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:51,500
with metallic pointed,
almost gothic styling.

203
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:53,167
His power things
are very flattering,

204
00:08:53,267 --> 00:08:55,500
never ever making him
look pudgy of bloated.

205
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:57,267
Mr. Crow?
CROW: Thank you.

206
00:08:57,367 --> 00:08:58,433
Mr. X, on the other hand,

207
00:08:58,533 --> 00:09:00,600
is wearing a white
cotton space-shift

208
00:09:00,700 --> 00:09:03,167
with almost
a nymph-like quality to it.

209
00:09:03,267 --> 00:09:04,600
It is belted and gathered
at the joints

210
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:06,900
to give it
a lighter than air feel.

211
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,500
You too can feel one sixth
your own weight

212
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:13,100
in this space-shift by Dundee.

213
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:15,267
JOEL:
Alien train supplied by Tyco.

214
00:09:16,767 --> 00:09:19,200
TOM:
Oh, he didn't come
out of his tuck in time.

215
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:22,800
CROW:
I'll take that, Mr. Bond.

216
00:09:23,833 --> 00:09:25,433
TOM:
Nipple, nipple, tweak, tweak,

217
00:09:25,533 --> 00:09:28,100
fly, fly, fly!

218
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:33,100
JOEL:
Oh I hate that,
my shoe's still untied.

219
00:09:34,900 --> 00:09:37,800
CROW:
How come he doesn't land
on his head?

220
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:38,800
JOEL:
He knows better.

221
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:40,200
TOM:
He'd stick there
like a lawn dart.

222
00:09:40,300 --> 00:09:42,233
- ...found our ship.
I took care of him,

223
00:09:42,333 --> 00:09:44,133
but I'm afraid
he already reported us.

224
00:09:44,233 --> 00:09:46,200
How much longer
before we can take off?

225
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:48,267
- One of those tanks
is headed this way!

226
00:09:50,067 --> 00:09:51,100
- How 'bout it, Hank?

227
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:53,167
- I won't guarantee the results
but we can try.

228
00:09:53,267 --> 00:09:54,900
Here it goes, all jets.

229
00:09:56,133 --> 00:09:57,333
CROW:
Well, one jet.

230
00:10:07,367 --> 00:10:09,400
JOEL:
Here it comes.

231
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:11,200
- Looks like we may make it.

232
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:13,567
- If we get out of range
of their ray gun in time.

233
00:10:32,133 --> 00:10:34,667
- Shut off
that induction valve!

234
00:10:34,767 --> 00:10:36,733
CROW: Fire in the hole.
JOEL: We're hit!

235
00:10:36,833 --> 00:10:38,133
Everybody lean.

236
00:10:38,233 --> 00:10:40,200
TOM:
Medic, oh!

237
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:43,967
Looks like
the Macy Parade gone awry.

238
00:10:44,067 --> 00:10:45,367
- Particulate compartments.

239
00:10:45,467 --> 00:10:46,467
- Right.

240
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:54,367
TOM:
Kablooey.

241
00:10:54,467 --> 00:10:55,833
CROW:
Taking a lot of flack.

242
00:10:55,933 --> 00:10:58,067
JOEL:
Kind of reminds me of Das Boot.

243
00:10:58,167 --> 00:10:59,167
- Looks like
we're in the clear.

244
00:10:59,267 --> 00:11:00,967
TOM: Das "Moon" Boot.
JOEL: Yeah.

245
00:11:01,067 --> 00:11:01,967
- Things are sort of
messed up in there,

246
00:11:02,067 --> 00:11:03,067
but nothing serious.

247
00:11:03,167 --> 00:11:04,467
CROW:
Just everything's broken.

248
00:11:04,567 --> 00:11:05,633
- Okay, Hank.

249
00:11:05,733 --> 00:11:07,067
You can start
for the Earth now.

250
00:11:07,167 --> 00:11:09,700
Make a 180 degree turn.

251
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:12,867
TOM: 180 degrees?
JOEL: For savings!

252
00:11:14,367 --> 00:11:16,600
[CROW HOWLS LIKE A WOLF]

253
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:19,700
[♪♪♪]

254
00:11:29,700 --> 00:11:30,667
- Very well.

255
00:11:30,767 --> 00:11:32,400
TOM:
A princess phone, how cute.

256
00:11:32,500 --> 00:11:35,567
- Patrol car reported
that the spaceship has escaped.

257
00:11:35,667 --> 00:11:38,600
- Ship must not be allowed
to land on Earth.

258
00:11:42,633 --> 00:11:44,800
JOEL:
Set it for stun.

259
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:46,900
CROW:
Why does the Earth
have a shadow?

260
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:48,500
TOM:
Why are there clouds in space?

261
00:11:48,600 --> 00:11:49,900
CROW:
Why are we watching this?

262
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,533
TOM:
Daddy, what's Vietnam?

263
00:11:52,633 --> 00:11:54,600
JOEL:
I told you
to never call me here.

264
00:11:54,700 --> 00:11:57,600
- ...rocket ship has escaped
and is headed back to Earth.

265
00:11:57,700 --> 00:12:00,100
He has all the information
about our planned invasion

266
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:02,900
and must not be allowed
to tell it to the authorities.

267
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,400
Will you have a ray gun
completed by the time he lands?

268
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:08,533
- I'm afraid that is
impossible, Your Excellency.

269
00:12:08,633 --> 00:12:11,533
CROW:
All the hardware stores
are closed.

270
00:12:11,633 --> 00:12:15,433
- That ship must be destroyed
before anyone can get out of it.

271
00:12:15,533 --> 00:12:17,500
JOEL:
Set that on stun.

272
00:12:20,367 --> 00:12:21,900
- You heard the orders.

273
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:23,833
- Yeah,
but it might not be that easy.

274
00:12:23,933 --> 00:12:25,633
There might be guards
at the landing field.

275
00:12:25,733 --> 00:12:28,067
- Nevertheless, you will
carry out instructions.

276
00:12:28,167 --> 00:12:30,300
I will furnish you
with a bomb.

277
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:31,767
You can devise some means
for placing it

278
00:12:31,867 --> 00:12:34,200
under the ship
the moment it lands.

279
00:12:34,300 --> 00:12:35,900
- How do we know
when they'll get here?

280
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,267
- I'll pick up their approach
on my radar.

281
00:12:38,367 --> 00:12:41,500
You can be in position
when they land.

282
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:42,600
JOEL:
I can't believe those thugs

283
00:12:42,700 --> 00:12:45,433
are taking orders from
a guy dressed in spandex.

284
00:12:45,533 --> 00:12:47,067
CROW:
Eh.

285
00:12:47,167 --> 00:12:48,467
Hey, great spot for a picnic.

286
00:12:48,567 --> 00:12:50,367
Did you bring the basket?

287
00:12:50,467 --> 00:12:51,367
TOM:
Yeah, I brought it.

288
00:12:51,467 --> 00:12:52,600
Fried chicken, right?

289
00:12:52,700 --> 00:12:54,300
CROW:
Crouch down like me.

290
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:56,267
JOEL:
We supposed to run
like this the whole way?

291
00:12:56,367 --> 00:12:57,367
CROW:
Yeah.

292
00:13:01,900 --> 00:13:03,700
JOEL:
Couple of dumb cops.

293
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:06,300
- Yes. Got to blast 'em.

294
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:07,933
TOM:
There's gum under this one.

295
00:13:08,033 --> 00:13:09,333
- ...get out of the ship.

296
00:13:13,067 --> 00:13:14,067
- Here she comes.

297
00:13:15,467 --> 00:13:18,467
JOEL:
Look at the lines
on that beauty.

298
00:13:18,567 --> 00:13:20,000
- Coming in for a landing.

299
00:13:24,433 --> 00:13:25,800
JOEL:
Just keep turning
and switching knobs

300
00:13:25,900 --> 00:13:27,533
'til we get her down.

301
00:13:30,367 --> 00:13:33,100
- Calling landing fields.
Calling landing fields.

302
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:35,000
- Landing fields
to rocket, come in.

303
00:13:35,100 --> 00:13:36,000
- We're coming down.

304
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:37,000
Field clear?

305
00:13:37,100 --> 00:13:39,133
CROW:
Walkie-talkie pickin' up, pal?

306
00:13:44,067 --> 00:13:46,900
- Alright, Hank,
make your 180 degree turn.

307
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:48,400
JOEL:
Everybody lean.

308
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:56,133
- You take the one
on the right.

309
00:13:57,367 --> 00:13:58,267
JOEL:
The big one?

310
00:13:58,367 --> 00:14:00,067
CROW:
He's not my type.

311
00:14:07,467 --> 00:14:08,667
TOM:
Oh! There's gunplay.

312
00:14:10,733 --> 00:14:12,000
CROW:
I wanna hear a ricochet.

313
00:14:12,100 --> 00:14:14,800
JOEL:
Why is it darker on the Earth
than on the Moon?

314
00:14:14,900 --> 00:14:16,100
TOM:
A humanist question,

315
00:14:16,200 --> 00:14:18,267
or a question
a human might ask?

316
00:14:21,667 --> 00:14:23,200
JOEL:
They're at the Cadillac ranch.

317
00:14:29,367 --> 00:14:30,633
CROW:
He's at a bang bang.

318
00:14:34,567 --> 00:14:36,967
TOM:
What kind of a welcome party
is this?

319
00:14:37,067 --> 00:14:39,033
CROW:
I hope this doesn't happen
at the hotel.

320
00:14:39,133 --> 00:14:40,467
Did you call ahead?

321
00:14:48,367 --> 00:14:49,500
Pa-too-ee.

322
00:14:49,600 --> 00:14:50,667
- Let's get over to the car.

323
00:14:50,767 --> 00:14:52,767
TOM:
Yeah, the bullets
don't hurt over there.

324
00:15:00,967 --> 00:15:02,733
JOEL:
Get over here.

325
00:15:02,833 --> 00:15:04,267
TOM:
Okay, sir.

326
00:15:07,100 --> 00:15:09,133
Well, you'd notice
by all the [indistinct].

327
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:14,100
- They're taking off in a car.

328
00:15:14,200 --> 00:15:16,500
I'll go after 'em,
you guard the ship.

329
00:15:16,600 --> 00:15:17,500
JOEL:
I'll go after 'em

330
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:19,267
and leave you guys stranded.

331
00:15:19,367 --> 00:15:20,367
CROW:
Thanks.

332
00:15:21,967 --> 00:15:23,200
Let's hold each other.

333
00:15:32,667 --> 00:15:34,433
- They're after us,
step on it.

334
00:15:35,633 --> 00:15:38,200
CROW:
Hey, you almost lost him
in that last edit.

335
00:15:44,633 --> 00:15:47,033
TOM:
Meanwhile,
hundreds of miles away...

336
00:15:47,133 --> 00:15:48,133
CROW:
Good driving.

337
00:15:51,867 --> 00:15:54,000
- He's hanging onto us.
Give it all you can.

338
00:15:54,100 --> 00:15:56,867
TOM:
Nothing's worth
driving like that.

339
00:15:56,967 --> 00:15:59,100
JOEL:
They don't even have
their belts on, I bet.

340
00:15:59,200 --> 00:16:00,967
TOM:
Nice shot of me.

341
00:16:14,700 --> 00:16:15,733
Whoa.

342
00:16:15,833 --> 00:16:18,267
Another good shot of me.
I look like Gene Hackman.

343
00:16:21,833 --> 00:16:22,833
- Stop on that bridge.

344
00:16:22,933 --> 00:16:24,733
JOEL:
He's got an idea.

345
00:16:24,833 --> 00:16:25,733
CROW:
Hey, wait. Didn't you see

346
00:16:25,833 --> 00:16:27,833
the "bridge of death"
sign up there?

347
00:16:37,267 --> 00:16:38,267
TOM:
Oh, litter. That'll do it.

348
00:16:38,367 --> 00:16:39,900
Cody hates it when we litter.

349
00:16:41,300 --> 00:16:44,033
JOEL:
Uh, you shouldn't drink
before you have a car chase.

350
00:16:45,133 --> 00:16:46,900
TOM:
Let's put this garbage here,

351
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,300
Cody'll see it,
he'll stop, we'll get away.

352
00:16:55,333 --> 00:16:56,233
We'd better go.

353
00:16:56,333 --> 00:16:57,467
[ALL TALKING AT ONCE]

354
00:16:57,567 --> 00:16:58,867
CROW:
I want to see how it ends.

355
00:16:58,967 --> 00:16:59,967
JOEL:
Come on, let's go.

356
00:17:00,067 --> 00:17:01,200
CROW:
I want to see this.

357
00:17:01,300 --> 00:17:02,200
Oh, come on.

358
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:03,833
TOM:
Think Cody'll survive?

359
00:17:23,900 --> 00:17:25,667
[HUMMING]

360
00:17:25,767 --> 00:17:27,200
- Hey, whatta ya reading?

361
00:17:27,300 --> 00:17:29,833
- Oh, it's the latest edition
of "Tiger Bot Magazine."

362
00:17:29,933 --> 00:17:32,667
They have a big full color
spread of Data from Star Trek.

363
00:17:32,767 --> 00:17:35,833
- Data, wow!
He's dreamy.

364
00:17:35,933 --> 00:17:38,600
- Says here he's getting bigger
than Kirk Cameron too.

365
00:17:38,700 --> 00:17:41,967
- Wow! Hey, hey,
look at that schematic there.

366
00:17:42,067 --> 00:17:43,733
Wow, look at that brain.

367
00:17:43,833 --> 00:17:46,267
- Wish I had me one of
those positronic brains.

368
00:17:46,367 --> 00:17:48,500
- Wow, Data's got
all the candy.

369
00:17:48,600 --> 00:17:51,500
Hey Servo, uh,
who's the dream date this week?

370
00:17:51,600 --> 00:17:52,633
- Let me see...

371
00:17:52,733 --> 00:17:54,700
says here...

372
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:55,967
Tweaky.

373
00:17:56,067 --> 00:17:58,467
- Tweaky!
What a coaster.

374
00:17:58,567 --> 00:18:00,367
He hasn't worked
since Buck Rogers.

375
00:18:00,467 --> 00:18:01,967
- I know, what a putz.

376
00:18:02,067 --> 00:18:04,467
Bidi-bidi-bidi,
wanna dance?

377
00:18:04,567 --> 00:18:05,467
Buck.

378
00:18:05,567 --> 00:18:06,500
[LAUGHS]
- Yeah.

379
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:07,933
You know, that guy
couldn't interface

380
00:18:08,033 --> 00:18:09,700
without a load pan adapter.

381
00:18:09,800 --> 00:18:11,533
Such a shrimpy
little bot anyway

382
00:18:11,633 --> 00:18:13,267
with those ineffectual arms

383
00:18:13,367 --> 00:18:16,100
and that stupid
bubble head, and...

384
00:18:16,200 --> 00:18:17,400
- Funny, Crow.

385
00:18:17,500 --> 00:18:19,167
- Whoops.

386
00:18:19,267 --> 00:18:21,667
- Oh, great! Look,
here's the spreadsheets on Data.

387
00:18:21,767 --> 00:18:24,833
Let's see, turn ons,
electricity,

388
00:18:24,933 --> 00:18:28,933
quiet walks in the holodeck,
cray supercomputers.

389
00:18:29,033 --> 00:18:31,033
Let's see, turn offs,
switches...

390
00:18:31,133 --> 00:18:33,233
- Yeah.
- ...fat Klingons in sweatpants

391
00:18:33,333 --> 00:18:36,400
and Shatner hanging around
the set telling old war stories.

392
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:38,567
Oh, here, and Tandy products.

393
00:18:38,667 --> 00:18:41,767
Okay, pet peeves,
yes, definitely.

394
00:18:41,867 --> 00:18:43,933
Favorite actress,
Julie Newmar.

395
00:18:44,033 --> 00:18:46,400
Favorite Scotch,
James Doohan.

396
00:18:46,500 --> 00:18:47,500
Secret fantasy--

397
00:18:47,600 --> 00:18:49,000
hey, wait a minute,
look at this!

398
00:18:49,100 --> 00:18:50,800
His secret fantasy
says right here,

399
00:18:50,900 --> 00:18:53,767
"I'd really love to be a human."
Oh, man!

400
00:18:53,867 --> 00:18:55,367
- Aw, how predictable.

401
00:18:55,467 --> 00:18:57,133
The old Pinocchio syndrome.

402
00:18:57,233 --> 00:18:58,967
- This is the kind of
clown that gives us bots

403
00:18:59,067 --> 00:19:00,200
a really bad name.

404
00:19:00,300 --> 00:19:02,400
- Yeah, what a sellout.

405
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:04,133
- And speaking of sellouts.

406
00:19:04,233 --> 00:19:07,233
[♪♪♪]

407
00:19:18,233 --> 00:19:19,867
TOM:
I just want to read
the article on Data.

408
00:19:19,967 --> 00:19:21,867
JOEL:
I don't want you
reading this stuff.

409
00:19:21,967 --> 00:19:24,133
TOM:
Hey, you're paying for that.

410
00:19:24,233 --> 00:19:26,667
CROW:
Hey, litterbug, litterbug.

411
00:19:26,767 --> 00:19:27,667
TOM:
[indistinct]

412
00:19:27,767 --> 00:19:29,200
JOEL:
♪ A corpse is a horse ♪

413
00:19:29,300 --> 00:19:32,533
♪ A corpse a corpse
Unless it... ♪

414
00:19:32,633 --> 00:19:34,900
CROW:
It's The Little Rascals
music again.

415
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:36,800
JOEL:
Yeah.

416
00:19:36,900 --> 00:19:38,800
Hey, look at the shadow
I cast, you guys.

417
00:19:38,900 --> 00:19:39,867
CROW:
Wow.

418
00:19:39,967 --> 00:19:41,367
TOM:
I think you're
casting credits, too.

419
00:19:41,467 --> 00:19:42,667
CROW:
Hey, do the rabbit.

420
00:19:45,300 --> 00:19:46,200
JOEL:
Naw, I can't do that.

421
00:19:46,300 --> 00:19:48,700
CROW:
Eh, looked more like
Abe Lincoln.

422
00:19:48,800 --> 00:19:50,500
TOM:
Or Abe Vigoda really.

423
00:19:57,967 --> 00:19:59,600
TOM:
Wallace Fox,
I'd like to hunt him down

424
00:19:59,700 --> 00:20:02,167
and skin him
and make him into a little rug.

425
00:20:02,267 --> 00:20:03,467
JOEL:
I'll get it.

426
00:20:04,667 --> 00:20:06,533
CROW:
Hey, why is the groom
looking at us?

427
00:20:06,633 --> 00:20:08,067
TOM:
He's never been
in a movie before.

428
00:20:08,167 --> 00:20:09,533
JOEL:
Hey Doc,
he's gonna see us.

429
00:20:09,633 --> 00:20:13,833
- ...lawfully wedded husband,
to love, honor and obey,

430
00:20:13,933 --> 00:20:16,000
until death do you part?

431
00:20:16,100 --> 00:20:17,500
- I-- I do.

432
00:20:17,600 --> 00:20:18,500
[CRIES OUT]

433
00:20:18,600 --> 00:20:19,500
CROW:
Nice die.

434
00:20:19,600 --> 00:20:21,500
TOM:
You may now bury the bride.

435
00:20:27,667 --> 00:20:29,400
- Take her into the anteroom.

436
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:31,133
- Yes, sir.
CROW: And keep it quiet.

437
00:20:31,233 --> 00:20:32,800
People want
to take their gifts back

438
00:20:32,900 --> 00:20:34,133
if they hear about this.

439
00:20:34,233 --> 00:20:36,400
[CHATTERING]

440
00:20:36,500 --> 00:20:37,867
JOEL:
It ruins so many wedding nights

441
00:20:37,967 --> 00:20:40,100
when the bride dies.

442
00:20:40,200 --> 00:20:43,200
- Please,
this is no time for interviews.

443
00:20:43,300 --> 00:20:45,000
- Shall I telephone
for the ambulance?

444
00:20:45,100 --> 00:20:48,500
- No, the undertaker.
She's dead.

445
00:20:48,600 --> 00:20:51,500
CROW:
Whoa, must be the father
of the bride, Gloomy Gus.

446
00:20:51,600 --> 00:20:52,733
TOM:
Sensitive guy.

447
00:20:55,067 --> 00:20:56,467
JOEL:
Oh man, that's so sad.

448
00:20:56,567 --> 00:20:58,767
Look, they're taking
the cake back.

449
00:20:58,867 --> 00:21:01,333
TOM:
Thank you, I love cake.

450
00:21:01,433 --> 00:21:04,700
Ooo, the frosting
is delicious!

451
00:21:06,033 --> 00:21:09,433
Alright, we got the cake,
now step on it.

452
00:21:09,533 --> 00:21:11,433
CROW:
Step on the cake, ew.

453
00:21:11,533 --> 00:21:14,167
TOM:
No, the car-- you!

454
00:21:18,233 --> 00:21:19,233
JOEL:
Bing.

455
00:21:29,100 --> 00:21:30,267
- Hey, all of you can go now.

456
00:21:30,367 --> 00:21:32,233
TOM:
Wow, life savers, thanks.

457
00:21:32,333 --> 00:21:34,567
- Gee, thanks.

458
00:21:34,667 --> 00:21:35,800
JOEL:
Lucky guy.

459
00:21:38,233 --> 00:21:40,133
CROW:
Hey, the red ones are missing.

460
00:21:40,233 --> 00:21:43,333
- Good evening, Dr. Rayburn.
I'm from the Forest Mortuary.

461
00:21:43,433 --> 00:21:45,000
- Forest Mortuary?

462
00:21:45,100 --> 00:21:46,833
But two of your men
just drove away

463
00:21:46,933 --> 00:21:48,433
with the body
a few moments ago.

464
00:21:48,533 --> 00:21:50,067
TOM: Huh?
- There must be some mistake.

465
00:21:50,167 --> 00:21:51,067
They weren't our men.

466
00:21:51,167 --> 00:21:52,467
- Well, I've heard of
ambulance chasing

467
00:21:52,567 --> 00:21:53,867
but this is something new.

468
00:21:53,967 --> 00:21:55,400
- New? It's sensational.

469
00:21:55,500 --> 00:21:58,200
Another kidnapping
of a dead bride, what a story!

470
00:21:58,300 --> 00:22:00,767
JOEL:
What a sensitive pair.

471
00:22:00,867 --> 00:22:01,767
CROW:
Yeah.

472
00:22:01,867 --> 00:22:03,867
TOM:
Body of dead bride disappears.

473
00:22:04,967 --> 00:22:07,233
Audience baffled
by free floating headlines.

474
00:22:07,333 --> 00:22:10,100
Trite headline device
used to enhance plot.

475
00:22:10,200 --> 00:22:11,733
Producers hope to work
in calendar flipping

476
00:22:11,833 --> 00:22:13,533
before the end of the film.

477
00:22:17,967 --> 00:22:19,600
- And a few minutes ago,
one of the men

478
00:22:19,700 --> 00:22:21,333
who helped remove
the body of the...

479
00:22:21,433 --> 00:22:23,167
TOM:
Equally trite
radio announcer device.

480
00:22:23,267 --> 00:22:24,733
- ...was apprehended
by the police.

481
00:22:24,833 --> 00:22:27,300
- Go on. Keep talking.

482
00:22:27,400 --> 00:22:28,300
- But I told you I was standing

483
00:22:28,400 --> 00:22:30,033
on the corner
minding my own business

484
00:22:30,133 --> 00:22:32,200
when along comes
the stiff wagon.

485
00:22:32,300 --> 00:22:33,833
A guy pokes his head
out of the window

486
00:22:33,933 --> 00:22:35,933
and asked me if I wanted
to make a couple of bucks.

487
00:22:36,033 --> 00:22:37,300
CROW:
And some Lifesavers.

488
00:22:37,400 --> 00:22:39,400
- Two. At least
all I could see.

489
00:22:39,500 --> 00:22:40,733
- What'd they look like?

490
00:22:40,833 --> 00:22:42,933
- I don't know.
It was dark.

491
00:22:43,033 --> 00:22:44,500
I didn't pay no attention
to faces.

492
00:22:44,600 --> 00:22:46,400
- You notice the license?

493
00:22:46,500 --> 00:22:48,167
- What with two bucks
staring me in the face?

494
00:22:48,267 --> 00:22:51,067
- Come on, tell the truth.
What'd you do with the corpse?

495
00:22:51,167 --> 00:22:53,233
- I didn't do nothing with it.

496
00:22:53,333 --> 00:22:55,867
JOEL:
He's gonna need that two bucks
to buy a new hat.

497
00:22:55,967 --> 00:22:56,967
CROW:
Yeah.

498
00:22:58,167 --> 00:23:00,100
TOM:
Shadowy corpse thief escapes.

499
00:23:01,500 --> 00:23:03,833
Trite headline device
still ineffective.

500
00:23:05,333 --> 00:23:06,533
Building still large.

501
00:23:06,633 --> 00:23:08,033
- As District Attorney,
Mrs. Wentworth,

502
00:23:08,133 --> 00:23:09,833
I assure you
there won't be any trouble.

503
00:23:09,933 --> 00:23:11,833
You can proceed
with the wedding as planned.

504
00:23:11,933 --> 00:23:14,467
- Well, we want some
assurance besides words.

505
00:23:14,567 --> 00:23:15,633
We want protection.

506
00:23:15,733 --> 00:23:16,667
JOEL:
Aw, you shoulda discussed that

507
00:23:16,767 --> 00:23:18,667
with your daughter
earlier.

508
00:23:18,767 --> 00:23:20,533
- And if I'm any judge of
the appearance of your daughter,

509
00:23:20,633 --> 00:23:22,667
she looks as healthy
as she is beautiful.

510
00:23:22,767 --> 00:23:23,833
- Oh really, mother.

511
00:23:23,933 --> 00:23:26,367
I think you're making
much ado about nothing.

512
00:23:26,467 --> 00:23:27,633
CROW:
Hey, I thought
Shakespeare wrote that.

513
00:23:27,733 --> 00:23:29,033
- Not in the least.

514
00:23:29,133 --> 00:23:30,667
- Well, if we have protection,

515
00:23:30,767 --> 00:23:32,800
I'll feel
much better about it.

516
00:23:32,900 --> 00:23:34,733
- You'll have it alright,
Mrs. Wentworth.

517
00:23:38,700 --> 00:23:40,033
JOEL:
Boy, what a couple
of ugly hats,

518
00:23:40,133 --> 00:23:41,133
call the fashion police.

519
00:23:41,233 --> 00:23:42,833
- ...wait, I'll pick him up.

520
00:23:43,967 --> 00:23:45,700
- That's the Wentworth
mother and daughter, isn't it?

521
00:23:45,800 --> 00:23:46,800
Is the wedding called off?

522
00:23:46,900 --> 00:23:47,800
- Certainly not.

523
00:23:47,900 --> 00:23:49,133
And there isn't gonna be
any trouble either.

524
00:23:49,233 --> 00:23:50,667
- That's what
all the rest of them thought.

525
00:23:50,767 --> 00:23:52,367
- I can't answer for
Miss Wentworth's health

526
00:23:52,467 --> 00:23:54,033
but your paper
can print one thing,

527
00:23:54,133 --> 00:23:56,833
and that is that there'll be
no more theft of girls' bodies.

528
00:23:56,933 --> 00:23:59,167
CROW [WOMAN'S VOICE]:
Well, we shall see.

529
00:24:03,233 --> 00:24:05,633
- Yeah, this will get you
and Sandy into the wedding

530
00:24:05,733 --> 00:24:07,500
and I want plenty
of photographs.

531
00:24:07,600 --> 00:24:08,700
- Ho ho, don't worry, boss.

532
00:24:08,800 --> 00:24:10,733
I got enough film
to cover the Russian front.

533
00:24:10,833 --> 00:24:12,367
TOM:
But he'll get chocolate
on the pictures.

534
00:24:12,467 --> 00:24:14,133
- ...to take me
off the society column.

535
00:24:14,233 --> 00:24:16,333
- Oh, quit beefing.
You're getting a break.

536
00:24:16,433 --> 00:24:18,300
This is the most exclusive
wedding of the year.

537
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:20,400
- What if the Wentworth girl
drops dead too?

538
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:21,900
- You'll still tell me
what she's wearing

539
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:23,500
along with the rest
of the fancy pants.

540
00:24:23,600 --> 00:24:24,667
JOEL:
That's cold.

541
00:24:24,767 --> 00:24:25,667
- Get me the guest list

542
00:24:25,767 --> 00:24:27,000
and don't bother
about anything else.

543
00:24:27,100 --> 00:24:28,567
JOEL: Let me get that for you.
- ...with the police,

544
00:24:28,667 --> 00:24:30,367
and they'll be plenty more
thereabouts.

545
00:24:30,467 --> 00:24:32,000
And if the bride
holds up this time,

546
00:24:32,100 --> 00:24:33,700
there won't be any snatching.

547
00:24:33,800 --> 00:24:35,700
Now get going.
- What if I get a clue.

548
00:24:35,800 --> 00:24:37,933
- Oh, I wouldn't let
that bother me, sweetie pie.

549
00:24:38,033 --> 00:24:40,833
You wouldn't know a clue
if it bit you!

550
00:24:40,933 --> 00:24:43,000
- I'll remember
those words, stooge.

551
00:24:43,100 --> 00:24:46,300
CROW: [MAKES SOUNDS LIKE
THE THREE STOOGES CURLY]

552
00:24:46,400 --> 00:24:49,300
♪ I'm getting buried
In the morning ♪

553
00:24:49,400 --> 00:24:51,067
♪ Buried dead
Getting buried... ♪

554
00:24:51,167 --> 00:24:52,733
JOEL: Good one.
- ...nervous mother.

555
00:24:52,833 --> 00:24:55,267
After all, I'm the one
that's getting married.

556
00:24:55,367 --> 00:24:56,600
- Oh, I just can't help it.

557
00:24:56,700 --> 00:24:59,300
I'm so afraid
something will go wrong.

558
00:24:59,400 --> 00:25:03,000
- Oh, forget it.
Nothing can possibly happen.

559
00:25:03,100 --> 00:25:04,500
TOM:
Shhh, donk. Dadadada...

560
00:25:04,600 --> 00:25:06,833
- Oh, Allen!
It's perfect...

561
00:25:06,933 --> 00:25:08,100
JOEL:
Oh hi.

562
00:25:08,200 --> 00:25:09,733
You're gonna make
a perfect dead bride...

563
00:25:09,833 --> 00:25:10,800
TOM:
Keep a section of the dress.

564
00:25:10,900 --> 00:25:12,300
You'll need them
for your toes.

565
00:25:12,400 --> 00:25:13,767
JOEL:
Same fabric in the coffin.

566
00:25:17,967 --> 00:25:19,167
JOEL:
I get your car for you, sir?

567
00:25:19,267 --> 00:25:20,767
- ...and this wedding
won't wait for us.

568
00:25:20,867 --> 00:25:22,700
- Can I help it
if we had a blowout?

569
00:25:22,800 --> 00:25:23,767
- Serves you right.

570
00:25:23,867 --> 00:25:25,833
Why don't you buy
some new tires for this bug?

571
00:25:25,933 --> 00:25:27,667
- You know, I've been
thinking of getting--

572
00:25:27,767 --> 00:25:28,967
hey, are you kidding?

573
00:25:29,067 --> 00:25:30,267
ALL:
Oh! Doh!

574
00:25:30,367 --> 00:25:31,833
JOEL:
I can't believe he went
for that blowout story.

575
00:25:31,933 --> 00:25:33,967
He really is a stooge.

576
00:25:34,067 --> 00:25:36,233
[CROW IMITATES CURLY]

577
00:25:38,767 --> 00:25:39,933
TOM:
Keep your space cushion,

578
00:25:40,033 --> 00:25:42,533
check your blind spot, hands
at ten and two on the wheel.

579
00:25:42,633 --> 00:25:44,367
CROW:
Five minutes, Mr. Lugosi.

580
00:25:44,467 --> 00:25:48,200
JOEL [IMITATES BELA LUGOSI]:
I'm going to jump
out of this truck

581
00:25:48,300 --> 00:25:52,133
and scare the pants
off everybody.

582
00:25:52,233 --> 00:25:53,300
Blah! Blah!

583
00:25:55,867 --> 00:25:57,333
[KNOCK ON DOOR]

584
00:25:57,433 --> 00:25:58,500
CROW:
Land shark.

585
00:26:01,933 --> 00:26:02,967
It's Nelson Eddy.

586
00:26:03,067 --> 00:26:04,733
- You mustn't come in here.
It's bad luck!

587
00:26:04,833 --> 00:26:06,267
- Aw, not even a peak?

588
00:26:06,367 --> 00:26:08,733
- Not even one.
You'll see me soon enough.

589
00:26:08,833 --> 00:26:09,733
- You see?

590
00:26:09,833 --> 00:26:11,233
TOM: Oh.
CROW: Simp.

591
00:26:13,767 --> 00:26:14,900
- Oh girls, do you mind

592
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:17,567
if I have these last few minutes
alone with Alice?

593
00:26:17,667 --> 00:26:20,433
[ALL GIRLS TALKING TOGETHER]

594
00:26:20,533 --> 00:26:22,467
[ALL COMMENTATORS
IMITATING GIRLS]

595
00:26:22,567 --> 00:26:24,167
JOEL:
...she's gonna die anyway.

596
00:26:24,267 --> 00:26:26,167
- Well, mother,
you're about to lose me.

597
00:26:26,267 --> 00:26:28,100
- I hope not forever, dear.

598
00:26:28,200 --> 00:26:29,500
- 'Course not.

599
00:26:29,600 --> 00:26:31,467
What are you talking about?

600
00:26:31,567 --> 00:26:33,433
- Do you feel all right, dear?

601
00:26:33,533 --> 00:26:36,300
- Certainly. I never
felt better in my life.

602
00:26:36,400 --> 00:26:38,100
You should forget
all that silly nonsense

603
00:26:38,200 --> 00:26:40,233
about those brides
dropping dead.

604
00:26:40,333 --> 00:26:42,033
[KNOCK ON DOOR]

605
00:26:42,133 --> 00:26:43,300
JOEL:
Telegram.

606
00:26:47,267 --> 00:26:49,333
- For Miss Wentworth.
- Oh, thank you.

607
00:26:49,433 --> 00:26:50,900
CROW:
Seems like
a nice enough fella...

608
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:52,633
ALL:
...at first!

609
00:26:54,100 --> 00:26:55,433
- From Dwight.
- No doubt.

610
00:26:55,533 --> 00:26:57,433
TOM:
It's a box lunch.

611
00:26:57,533 --> 00:26:58,600
I love corn beef.

612
00:26:59,867 --> 00:27:01,667
- Wear this next to
your heart, darling.

613
00:27:01,767 --> 00:27:02,767
All my love.

614
00:27:02,867 --> 00:27:04,067
[SIGHS]

615
00:27:06,067 --> 00:27:07,933
What an unusual orchid.

616
00:27:08,033 --> 00:27:10,333
CROW: That's the package.
- ...saw one like it before.

617
00:27:10,433 --> 00:27:11,400
It's lovely.

618
00:27:11,500 --> 00:27:12,700
- It's beautiful!

619
00:27:12,800 --> 00:27:14,367
ALL:
It's deadly!

620
00:27:15,933 --> 00:27:17,167
JOEL [IMITATES LUGOSI]:
Now for my famous

621
00:27:17,267 --> 00:27:18,900
metamorphosis illusion.

622
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:20,433
I entered the back
of the hearse,

623
00:27:20,533 --> 00:27:21,800
no one's looking, and...

624
00:27:21,900 --> 00:27:23,067
[KNOCK ON DOOR]

625
00:27:23,167 --> 00:27:24,867
CROW:
Pizza delivery.

626
00:27:27,567 --> 00:27:29,067
- The minister is ready,
Mrs. Wentworth.

627
00:27:29,167 --> 00:27:30,967
- Thank you.
CROW: Get out of here.

628
00:27:37,667 --> 00:27:39,067
- Say a little prayer, darling.

629
00:27:39,167 --> 00:27:40,067
JOEL: I'll readjust that.
CROW: Muzza wuzzawuzza.

630
00:27:40,167 --> 00:27:41,700
Fuzza wuzzawuzza.

631
00:27:41,800 --> 00:27:42,800
How's that?

632
00:27:43,933 --> 00:27:45,567
JOEL:
Mmm, coulda been longer.

633
00:27:45,667 --> 00:27:48,500
Do you promise to love,
cherish, obey,

634
00:27:48,600 --> 00:27:51,200
until death do you... huh?

635
00:27:51,300 --> 00:27:52,233
[CRIES OUT]

636
00:27:52,333 --> 00:27:53,433
CROW:
Nice die.

637
00:27:53,533 --> 00:27:55,900
- Alice, Alice, Alice!

638
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:57,967
Oh, darling,
darling, are you alright?

639
00:27:58,067 --> 00:28:01,133
[ALL COMMENTATORS MUMBLING]

640
00:28:01,233 --> 00:28:02,133
- It happened again.

641
00:28:02,233 --> 00:28:03,700
- I just got the picture
of the month.

642
00:28:03,800 --> 00:28:05,567
TOM:
For dead bride magazine.

643
00:28:06,600 --> 00:28:09,133
JOEL:
Oh man, they're taking
the deli tray away?

644
00:28:09,233 --> 00:28:11,300
I think that's so scary.

645
00:28:11,400 --> 00:28:12,300
CROW:
Yeah, but we can sell it

646
00:28:12,400 --> 00:28:14,233
to the Feinberg wedding.

647
00:28:14,333 --> 00:28:15,867
JOEL:
Speaking of selling...

648
00:28:15,967 --> 00:28:18,967
[♪♪♪]

649
00:28:35,900 --> 00:28:37,967
JOEL:
Paunch, John, follow 'em.

650
00:28:43,967 --> 00:28:46,200
TOM [IMITATES LUGOSI]:
Peak-a-boo, my little lamb chop.

651
00:28:46,300 --> 00:28:47,700
CROW:
What a bonehead.

652
00:28:52,600 --> 00:28:53,500
JOEL:
Can I get your door for you?

653
00:28:53,600 --> 00:28:54,500
- I just told the news
to Keenan,

654
00:28:54,600 --> 00:28:55,600
did you get anymore pictures?

655
00:28:55,700 --> 00:28:56,933
- Did I get pictures?

656
00:28:57,033 --> 00:28:59,700
I covered everything except
a close-up of this pretty posy.

657
00:28:59,800 --> 00:29:02,367
- Why, that looks like
the one the bride wore.

658
00:29:02,467 --> 00:29:03,667
What are you doing with it?

659
00:29:03,767 --> 00:29:04,767
- Presenting it
to you, sweetheart,

660
00:29:04,867 --> 00:29:07,733
as a token of my devotion.
Take a smell.

661
00:29:07,833 --> 00:29:10,200
- Who ever heard of
an orchid with a scent?

662
00:29:10,300 --> 00:29:11,867
- Well, that one has.

663
00:29:11,967 --> 00:29:13,667
Maybe it's a clue.

664
00:29:15,633 --> 00:29:17,300
- What a peculiar sweet odor.

665
00:29:17,400 --> 00:29:18,733
JOEL:
Oh, that's me.

666
00:29:18,833 --> 00:29:20,667
- Yeah, make a swell
romaine salad.

667
00:29:20,767 --> 00:29:23,333
JOEL:
What a strange sense of flavor.

668
00:29:26,733 --> 00:29:27,833
- Hey, Pat?

669
00:29:27,933 --> 00:29:29,600
You're not gonna
pass out too, are you?

670
00:29:29,700 --> 00:29:31,333
- No, no, I'm okay.

671
00:29:31,433 --> 00:29:33,600
TOM:
It's just the pace of this movie
that's got to me.

672
00:29:36,333 --> 00:29:37,867
JOEL: Need a push?
TOM: Okay.

673
00:29:40,700 --> 00:29:41,700
That's nutty.

674
00:29:44,067 --> 00:29:45,567
CROW:
Hey! It's a hotrod.

675
00:29:46,600 --> 00:29:48,067
- You stay right here.

676
00:29:55,100 --> 00:29:56,767
JOEL:
I gotta scare 'em away.

677
00:29:56,867 --> 00:29:57,767
CROW:
Good heater.

678
00:29:57,867 --> 00:29:58,867
JOEL:
You know, that wouldn't happen

679
00:29:58,967 --> 00:30:01,133
if he had thrush pipes on it,
I'm sure of it.

680
00:30:02,867 --> 00:30:05,167
[IMITATES LUGOSI]
Peak-a-boo, I see you.

681
00:30:05,267 --> 00:30:06,900
Are you frightened yet?

682
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:10,133
Grab the spare tire,
the toolbox, and the deli tray.

683
00:30:10,233 --> 00:30:11,933
Then we short-sheet the bed!

684
00:30:12,033 --> 00:30:13,833
I love these frat pranks.

685
00:30:17,167 --> 00:30:18,267
TOM:
Oh, so that's how they do

686
00:30:18,367 --> 00:30:19,933
the vanishing corpse trick.

687
00:30:20,033 --> 00:30:21,367
There's a secret panel
in the back

688
00:30:21,467 --> 00:30:23,233
and they take it out.

689
00:30:25,033 --> 00:30:26,467
I feel like I've been taken.

690
00:30:33,100 --> 00:30:35,933
- What kind of a gag is this?
No driver, no license plate.

691
00:30:36,033 --> 00:30:37,667
- Maybe it isn't a gag,
maybe it's a decoy.

692
00:30:37,767 --> 00:30:38,800
ALL:
Nah!

693
00:30:38,900 --> 00:30:40,833
CROW:
Yeah, but don't bother
putting it out.

694
00:30:47,500 --> 00:30:49,100
TOM: Wuh-oh.
JOEL: What's going on here?

695
00:30:49,200 --> 00:30:50,100
Check it out.

696
00:30:50,200 --> 00:30:51,700
TOM:
Two abandoned hearses
in one day?

697
00:30:51,800 --> 00:30:53,400
What are the odds
of that happening?

698
00:30:53,500 --> 00:30:55,767
- Calling all cars.
Calling all cars.

699
00:30:55,867 --> 00:30:58,800
Another abduction
of a girl's corpse.

700
00:30:58,900 --> 00:31:01,167
Brunette,
22 years of age,

701
00:31:01,267 --> 00:31:04,267
stop and search all trucks
and suspicious looking cars.

702
00:31:04,367 --> 00:31:05,633
CROW:
Be on the lookout
for my toupee.

703
00:31:05,733 --> 00:31:07,267
- That's all.
Burnside.

704
00:31:19,967 --> 00:31:20,867
- Carlo?

705
00:31:20,967 --> 00:31:22,600
We're taking a look
inside this rig.

706
00:31:22,700 --> 00:31:24,267
- Sure thing, officer.

707
00:31:24,367 --> 00:31:25,633
JOEL:
But I don't have
any dead bodies in back

708
00:31:25,733 --> 00:31:27,200
or anything if that's what
you're talking about.

709
00:31:27,300 --> 00:31:28,667
I don't mind showing you.

710
00:31:28,767 --> 00:31:30,633
Come around to the back here,
nothing wrong here.

711
00:31:30,733 --> 00:31:31,733
Check it out.

712
00:31:34,167 --> 00:31:35,233
TOM:
What's that then?

713
00:31:38,500 --> 00:31:40,167
Hey, I'm in here!

714
00:31:40,267 --> 00:31:42,367
JOEL:
Hey, they should check
underneath them.

715
00:31:45,133 --> 00:31:47,833
CROW:
Looks like
a driver's license photo.

716
00:31:47,933 --> 00:31:49,067
- Alright, go ahead.

717
00:31:54,900 --> 00:31:57,567
- Change that headline to read,
"Is Wedlock Challenged?"

718
00:31:57,667 --> 00:32:00,100
Then set up an editorial
attacking the DEA's office

719
00:32:00,200 --> 00:32:02,733
for playing marbles
while some madman runs away

720
00:32:02,833 --> 00:32:04,300
with the bodies
of dead brides.

721
00:32:04,400 --> 00:32:05,600
[JOEL'S MUFFLED SPEECH]

722
00:32:05,700 --> 00:32:07,000
- Send up the proofs
right away.

723
00:32:07,100 --> 00:32:08,300
CROW:
And get me a ham on rye.

724
00:32:08,400 --> 00:32:09,733
- So, it's you.

725
00:32:09,833 --> 00:32:11,433
Well,
of all the rotten stories

726
00:32:11,533 --> 00:32:12,900
that have ever been turned
into this office,

727
00:32:13,000 --> 00:32:14,400
yours takes the prize.

728
00:32:14,500 --> 00:32:15,400
No guest list,

729
00:32:15,500 --> 00:32:16,933
and we don't even know
what the bride wore.

730
00:32:17,033 --> 00:32:18,367
- She wore this orchid.

731
00:32:18,467 --> 00:32:21,767
- Listen, Miss Hunter,
I have a great responsibility.

732
00:32:21,867 --> 00:32:23,100
I'm a public servant.

733
00:32:23,200 --> 00:32:25,767
Parents are calling in
The Chronicle frantically

734
00:32:25,867 --> 00:32:27,800
with tears in their voice,

735
00:32:27,900 --> 00:32:29,667
begging for news
of their daughters.

736
00:32:29,767 --> 00:32:32,167
I have a daughter myself
who's about to be married.

737
00:32:32,267 --> 00:32:33,800
Maybe she'll drop dead too.

738
00:32:33,900 --> 00:32:36,167
But she won't drop dead
from an orchid.

739
00:32:36,267 --> 00:32:37,167
- I didn't say that.

740
00:32:37,267 --> 00:32:38,933
- Well, then
what are you talking about?

741
00:32:39,033 --> 00:32:40,833
- Every one of the brides
that died and disappeared

742
00:32:40,933 --> 00:32:42,233
were wearing
one of these orchids.

743
00:32:42,333 --> 00:32:44,433
- How do you know?
- Well, I checked.

744
00:32:44,533 --> 00:32:46,300
TOM: It's my job.
- Look at these pictures.

745
00:32:47,500 --> 00:32:49,733
- Alright, perhaps
it's a coincidence.

746
00:32:49,833 --> 00:32:51,800
It still doesn't
spell anything.

747
00:32:51,900 --> 00:32:54,067
- Then where did the
Wentworth girl get this orchid?

748
00:32:54,167 --> 00:32:55,600
The bridegroom
didn't give it to her.

749
00:32:55,700 --> 00:32:56,600
JOEL:
Huh?

750
00:32:56,700 --> 00:32:57,667
- ...a dozen people
gave it to her.

751
00:32:57,767 --> 00:32:59,433
Her mammy, her pappy.

752
00:32:59,533 --> 00:33:00,433
JOEL:
The guy she went out with.

753
00:33:00,533 --> 00:33:02,733
- Besides, this orchid
has peculiar odor.

754
00:33:02,833 --> 00:33:03,800
That's unusual.

755
00:33:03,900 --> 00:33:06,200
JOEL: That's me.
- ...why don't you follow it up?

756
00:33:06,300 --> 00:33:07,233
- Is that an assignment?

757
00:33:07,333 --> 00:33:08,333
- It's a notice
that you're through

758
00:33:08,433 --> 00:33:09,900
if you don't
bring something in.

759
00:33:10,000 --> 00:33:11,300
- Okay, Mr. Keenan.

760
00:33:12,733 --> 00:33:15,233
TOM:
Meanwhile,
back at the Adams Family home...

761
00:33:16,367 --> 00:33:17,800
CROW:
Oh, that must be Stanley,

762
00:33:17,900 --> 00:33:19,333
their garage door opener.

763
00:33:19,433 --> 00:33:21,900
Always trying to help
people do things right.

764
00:33:22,000 --> 00:33:24,767
[GIGGLE]
Everything... uh, ow.

765
00:33:29,467 --> 00:33:30,667
JOEL:
Bring it in.

766
00:33:40,100 --> 00:33:42,967
Hey, there's Billy Barty
and Cloris Leachman.

767
00:33:43,067 --> 00:33:43,967
TOM:
What's with the noise?

768
00:33:44,067 --> 00:33:45,867
You trying to wake the dead?

769
00:33:45,967 --> 00:33:48,367
CROW:
He doesn't have any pants on
underneath that thing.

770
00:33:50,033 --> 00:33:51,033
TOM:
I'm glad it's shut then.

771
00:33:51,133 --> 00:33:52,633
JOEL:
It's a soapbox racer.

772
00:33:54,267 --> 00:33:55,533
CROW:
Don't open it.

773
00:34:02,700 --> 00:34:04,600
- My little family.

774
00:34:04,700 --> 00:34:05,967
You're also very fateful.

775
00:34:06,067 --> 00:34:07,833
- Master,
the Countess is waiting.

776
00:34:07,933 --> 00:34:09,733
- Upstairs?
- There.

777
00:34:09,833 --> 00:34:12,100
- Oh, we must hurry.
Mike?

778
00:34:12,200 --> 00:34:14,367
JOEL:
That's one scary name, Mike.

779
00:34:14,467 --> 00:34:15,367
CROW:
Mike.

780
00:34:15,467 --> 00:34:17,700
JOEL:
The evil henchman, Mike.

781
00:34:17,800 --> 00:34:20,333
TOM:
Sends shivers up my diode.

782
00:34:25,467 --> 00:34:27,967
CROW:
Well, here comes
Meals On Wheels.

783
00:34:29,300 --> 00:34:31,400
JOEL:
Let me help you
with that there, mmm-hmm.

784
00:34:31,500 --> 00:34:34,233
CROW:
Oh Mike, posture, posture.

785
00:34:40,467 --> 00:34:42,500
TOM:
He has stone walls
painted on his drywall.

786
00:34:42,600 --> 00:34:44,700
[MOANING]

787
00:34:47,067 --> 00:34:49,800
CROW:
She's practicing for
her role as Camille.

788
00:34:51,033 --> 00:34:52,267
- Are you suffering,
my beloved.

789
00:34:52,367 --> 00:34:55,433
- Terribly!
You must hurry.

790
00:34:55,533 --> 00:34:57,467
[MOANS]
- I'm so sorry.

791
00:34:57,567 --> 00:34:59,433
But I was detained.

792
00:34:59,533 --> 00:35:01,867
Please forgive me.
- Forgive you?

793
00:35:01,967 --> 00:35:06,733
You loiter and waste time,
while I wait here dying.

794
00:35:06,833 --> 00:35:08,267
Dying!

795
00:35:08,367 --> 00:35:09,900
TOM:
Well, it'd make
for a shorter movie.

796
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:11,667
- I will not let you die.

797
00:35:11,767 --> 00:35:14,533
- Better death
than agony like this.

798
00:35:14,633 --> 00:35:15,733
JOEL:
No wonder
it's taking them so long,

799
00:35:15,833 --> 00:35:18,033
look how long
it takes them to dress.

800
00:35:18,133 --> 00:35:19,233
- Look at me, look at me!

801
00:35:19,333 --> 00:35:20,567
CROW:
Uh, no thanks. I just ate.

802
00:35:20,667 --> 00:35:22,367
- Now, will you hurry?

803
00:35:25,367 --> 00:35:27,533
JOEL:
See, he's still not done
bundling up.

804
00:35:27,633 --> 00:35:28,900
TOM:
Why is he putting on gloves?

805
00:35:29,000 --> 00:35:30,633
She's dead.
What's the point?

806
00:35:30,733 --> 00:35:32,167
JOEL:
Maybe
he's a performance artist.

807
00:35:32,267 --> 00:35:33,633
We don't know.

808
00:35:33,733 --> 00:35:35,267
CROW:
Hey, do you think
he's a real doctor?

809
00:35:35,367 --> 00:35:37,900
I don't see
no diplomas on his wall.

810
00:35:38,000 --> 00:35:39,667
TOM:
I think he went to Scare U.

811
00:35:39,767 --> 00:35:42,200
[MOANING]

812
00:35:52,700 --> 00:35:54,800
Free ear piercing with death.

813
00:35:56,333 --> 00:35:57,233
CROW:
He's doing the ear piercing

814
00:35:57,333 --> 00:35:59,367
with a conductor's punch,
though.

815
00:36:05,100 --> 00:36:06,633
TOM:
Yee.

816
00:36:06,733 --> 00:36:08,233
JOEL [IMITATES BUGOSI]:
Yes.

817
00:36:08,333 --> 00:36:11,367
That felt good.

818
00:36:14,200 --> 00:36:15,233
Ah.

819
00:36:15,333 --> 00:36:18,167
CROW:
All that just for
a little maple syrup.

820
00:36:18,267 --> 00:36:19,967
JOEL:
Now we can have
the rich taste of bride

821
00:36:20,067 --> 00:36:21,267
any time we want it.

822
00:36:21,367 --> 00:36:22,967
CROW:
You know, it's not just
for breakfast anymore.

823
00:36:23,067 --> 00:36:24,067
JOEL:
Right.

824
00:36:31,367 --> 00:36:32,700
[GROANING]

825
00:36:32,800 --> 00:36:35,533
TOM:
He never calls anymore.

826
00:36:35,633 --> 00:36:40,067
He just comes over
and shoots me with big needles.

827
00:36:40,167 --> 00:36:42,000
CROW:
She's a quart low.

828
00:36:42,100 --> 00:36:44,933
[GROANING]

829
00:36:48,733 --> 00:36:50,100
- Courage, my dear.
Courage.

830
00:36:50,200 --> 00:36:51,400
[GROANING]

831
00:37:07,800 --> 00:37:09,300
TOM:
Don't throw the cotton ball.

832
00:37:13,133 --> 00:37:15,700
CROW:
Now you might feel
a little sting.

833
00:37:15,800 --> 00:37:17,733
[SCREAMS]

834
00:37:17,833 --> 00:37:19,967
Okay, a big sting.

835
00:37:21,400 --> 00:37:25,900
JOEL:
Lovely... lovely.

836
00:37:27,700 --> 00:37:30,200
[GASPING]

837
00:37:33,633 --> 00:37:37,433
JOEL:
Lovely... lovely.

838
00:37:37,533 --> 00:37:38,633
TOM: [SINGING TO THE TUNE
OF "WHIP IT" BY DEVO]

839
00:37:38,733 --> 00:37:40,533
♪ When a hunchback
Comes along... ♪

840
00:37:40,633 --> 00:37:41,833
JOEL:
♪ You must whip him ♪

841
00:37:41,933 --> 00:37:43,600
TOM:
♪ He's playing with your
Corpse... ♪

842
00:37:43,700 --> 00:37:45,567
JOEL:
♪ Unless you whip him ♪

843
00:37:45,667 --> 00:37:47,100
TOM:
♪ He will not go away... ♪

844
00:37:47,200 --> 00:37:49,067
JOEL:
♪ You must whip him ♪

845
00:37:49,167 --> 00:37:51,500
TOM:
♪ Whip him whip your hunchback ♪

846
00:37:52,833 --> 00:37:54,500
CROW:
Well,
that'll straighten him out.

847
00:37:57,567 --> 00:37:59,700
- Why do you beat
my son so hard.

848
00:37:59,800 --> 00:38:01,167
- Because he's a beast,
an animal.

849
00:38:01,267 --> 00:38:03,300
And someday I should
like to destroy him.

850
00:38:03,400 --> 00:38:04,800
TOM: It's just a hunch.
JOEL: [LAUGHS] Good one.

851
00:38:04,900 --> 00:38:07,900
- My poor son.
Why was he ever born?

852
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:10,900
CROW:
'Cause you didn't play safely.

853
00:38:19,167 --> 00:38:21,200
- Can you bear
to look at me now?

854
00:38:21,300 --> 00:38:22,700
CROW:
He can even look at you bare.

855
00:38:22,800 --> 00:38:23,967
- You're beautiful.

856
00:38:24,067 --> 00:38:26,267
And I shall always
keep you that way.

857
00:38:27,600 --> 00:38:29,000
TOM:
Hasn't he heard of cryogenics?

858
00:38:29,100 --> 00:38:30,067
CROW:
Or saran wrap.

859
00:38:30,167 --> 00:38:31,467
JOEL:
Or Tupperware?

860
00:38:35,567 --> 00:38:37,267
- Does she live?

861
00:38:37,367 --> 00:38:40,133
- As long as
she can be of any use to us.

862
00:38:40,233 --> 00:38:41,200
- She's pretty.

863
00:38:41,300 --> 00:38:42,367
- Mmm.

864
00:38:42,467 --> 00:38:43,733
- Very young.

865
00:38:43,833 --> 00:38:45,500
- All of them must be.

866
00:38:45,600 --> 00:38:46,933
Come, my dear.

867
00:38:49,333 --> 00:38:51,733
JOEL:
Let's get out of here too.

868
00:38:51,833 --> 00:38:54,067
TOM:
What did they call her?

869
00:38:54,167 --> 00:38:57,167
[♪♪♪]

870
00:39:02,400 --> 00:39:05,533
[GASPING]

871
00:39:07,833 --> 00:39:09,667
- Oh, man!

872
00:39:15,033 --> 00:39:16,633
[MOANING]

873
00:39:17,733 --> 00:39:19,767
[PANTING]

874
00:39:23,367 --> 00:39:25,567
- Whoa, no!

875
00:39:32,800 --> 00:39:35,300
- Oooooo.

876
00:39:35,400 --> 00:39:36,933
Tag, you're it.

877
00:39:41,233 --> 00:39:42,300
- Hey, wait wait.
Wait, wait, wait.

878
00:39:42,400 --> 00:39:43,300
Come here,
come here, you guys.

879
00:39:43,400 --> 00:39:45,233
Come on back here.
- What do you want?

880
00:39:45,333 --> 00:39:46,800
- What's going on?
- Face, tag! You're it!

881
00:39:46,900 --> 00:39:47,800
[ALARM BUZZING]

882
00:39:47,900 --> 00:39:50,100
- Oh you!
- Movie sign!

883
00:39:57,467 --> 00:39:58,433
- None of the florists
I checked with

884
00:39:58,533 --> 00:40:00,367
ever saw an orchid like that.

885
00:40:00,467 --> 00:40:02,900
- Well, that's not surprising.
This particular...

886
00:40:03,000 --> 00:40:04,133
JOEL:
I tricked you guys.

887
00:40:04,233 --> 00:40:05,133
TOM:
You really skunked us.

888
00:40:05,233 --> 00:40:06,533
CROW:
You cheated.

889
00:40:06,633 --> 00:40:09,267
TOM:
Next time we play TV tag.

890
00:40:09,367 --> 00:40:10,400
- No.

891
00:40:10,500 --> 00:40:12,233
But if you wish to
find out more about it...

892
00:40:12,333 --> 00:40:14,533
JOEL:
Read about it at your library.

893
00:40:14,633 --> 00:40:16,233
- ...name of Lorenz.

894
00:40:16,333 --> 00:40:19,133
He was the one who originally
hybridized this plant,

895
00:40:19,233 --> 00:40:20,733
over in Europe somewhere.

896
00:40:20,833 --> 00:40:22,833
- But I just can't go to Europe,
just to...

897
00:40:22,933 --> 00:40:24,733
- That isn't necessary.

898
00:40:24,833 --> 00:40:26,500
He lives up at Brookdale now.

899
00:40:26,600 --> 00:40:27,633
TOM:
Oh, how convenient.

900
00:40:27,733 --> 00:40:28,967
- Thank you, Mr. Smith.

901
00:40:29,067 --> 00:40:30,233
- You're quite welcome.

902
00:40:30,333 --> 00:40:32,667
JOEL:
He lives at a mall?

903
00:40:32,767 --> 00:40:34,633
[BELL RINGING]

904
00:40:36,900 --> 00:40:39,267
- Looks like that box
got a coffin in it, Jack.

905
00:40:39,367 --> 00:40:42,300
- Sure it does.
JOEL: A box full of box.

906
00:40:42,400 --> 00:40:44,367
- What do you suppose
he wants with a coffin?

907
00:40:44,467 --> 00:40:45,367
- Search me.

908
00:40:45,467 --> 00:40:47,467
He's such a strange,
spooky guy.

909
00:40:47,567 --> 00:40:49,133
The further I stay
away from him, the better.

910
00:40:49,233 --> 00:40:50,167
- Me too.

911
00:40:50,267 --> 00:40:51,267
CROW:
What a goofball.

912
00:40:51,367 --> 00:40:52,500
- I'll take charge of this.

913
00:40:52,600 --> 00:40:53,700
- Go ahead.
Take it away.

914
00:40:53,800 --> 00:40:54,967
Glad to get rid of it.

915
00:40:55,967 --> 00:40:59,000
JOEL:
Something stupid
this way comes.

916
00:41:07,067 --> 00:41:08,000
- Taxi, lady?

917
00:41:08,100 --> 00:41:09,300
CROW:
No, it's a suitcase.

918
00:41:09,400 --> 00:41:10,533
- Right this way.

919
00:41:13,200 --> 00:41:14,100
Where to, Miss?

920
00:41:14,200 --> 00:41:15,567
- To the home
of Professor Lorenz.

921
00:41:15,667 --> 00:41:17,867
- Lorenz? I'm sorry,
I can't take you there.

922
00:41:17,967 --> 00:41:19,467
I'm outta gas.

923
00:41:19,567 --> 00:41:21,167
- You mean,
you won't take me?

924
00:41:21,267 --> 00:41:22,200
TOM:
Usually the "out of gas" thing

925
00:41:22,300 --> 00:41:24,100
comes later
in the relationship.

926
00:41:24,200 --> 00:41:25,900
- Is this the only taxi in town?

927
00:41:26,000 --> 00:41:27,500
- Yes, ma'am, it is.

928
00:41:27,600 --> 00:41:30,067
- Look, I've simply got to
get to the Lorenz place.

929
00:41:30,167 --> 00:41:31,367
I'll make...
JOEL: Listen, dope.

930
00:41:31,467 --> 00:41:33,300
You take her with you.

931
00:41:33,400 --> 00:41:35,667
- That truck's going up there.
You might get a ride with him.

932
00:41:35,767 --> 00:41:38,233
That is, if you don't mind
riding with a coffin.

933
00:41:38,333 --> 00:41:40,367
CROW:
Are you kidding? I love coffins.

934
00:41:40,467 --> 00:41:41,933
Point me at it.

935
00:41:45,000 --> 00:41:47,000
- Would you give me a lift
to the Lorenz place?

936
00:41:47,100 --> 00:41:48,167
- What do you want to
go there for?

937
00:41:48,267 --> 00:41:49,967
- To see the professor.
- Does he expect you?

938
00:41:50,067 --> 00:41:51,900
- No, but I...
- Then we can't take you.

939
00:41:52,000 --> 00:41:54,667
TOM:
Jeez, welcome to Snotty-dale.

940
00:41:55,867 --> 00:41:58,767
JOEL:
She's determined
to get on that truck.

941
00:41:58,867 --> 00:42:00,567
TOM:
Oh, I love those skirts.

942
00:42:02,167 --> 00:42:03,500
Really attractive.

943
00:42:08,067 --> 00:42:10,867
CROW:
♪ I'm a little teacup
Short and... ♪

944
00:42:10,967 --> 00:42:15,667
Hey! Boss, the plane,
the plane!

945
00:42:15,767 --> 00:42:18,400
I mean, uh,
the babe, the babe.

946
00:42:21,733 --> 00:42:23,200
JOEL:
I think you have to be
under 43 inches

947
00:42:23,300 --> 00:42:24,633
to ride on that ride.

948
00:42:24,733 --> 00:42:27,300
- Get off and stay off!

949
00:42:27,400 --> 00:42:29,367
- Look, I've got to see
Professor Lorenz,

950
00:42:29,467 --> 00:42:30,367
it's important.

951
00:42:30,467 --> 00:42:32,933
- I said you couldn't ride.

952
00:42:33,033 --> 00:42:34,833
CROW:
But she's got a ticket
to ride.

953
00:42:34,933 --> 00:42:36,000
TOM:
She don't care.

954
00:42:36,100 --> 00:42:37,133
JOEL:
It's an eTicket though,

955
00:42:37,233 --> 00:42:39,500
and you know how short
those eTicket rides are.

956
00:42:39,600 --> 00:42:41,067
- ...you got my bag!

957
00:42:41,167 --> 00:42:42,633
Oh.

958
00:42:42,733 --> 00:42:44,300
TOM:
Doh.

959
00:42:45,633 --> 00:42:47,433
And then frustration set in.

960
00:42:47,533 --> 00:42:48,833
JOEL:
Sheesh.

961
00:42:54,400 --> 00:42:56,200
TOM:
She looks as fresh
as a country lane

962
00:42:56,300 --> 00:42:58,767
after a fresh spring shower.

963
00:43:10,600 --> 00:43:12,033
- Would you mind
giving me a lift?

964
00:43:12,133 --> 00:43:13,467
- Certainly not.
Where are you going?

965
00:43:13,567 --> 00:43:15,800
- I'm trying to get to
the home of Professor Lorenz.

966
00:43:15,900 --> 00:43:17,533
- Well, you're in luck.
That's where I'm going.

967
00:43:17,633 --> 00:43:18,533
JOEL:
Hey, so are we!

968
00:43:18,633 --> 00:43:19,600
TOM:
Yeah, yeah.

969
00:43:19,700 --> 00:43:22,400
Professor Lorenz, yeah,
come in, yeah, yeah.

970
00:43:22,500 --> 00:43:23,567
CROW:
Great.

971
00:43:25,000 --> 00:43:26,367
- My name is Dr. Foster.

972
00:43:26,467 --> 00:43:27,500
- I'm Patricia Hunter.

973
00:43:27,600 --> 00:43:28,767
JOEL: I'm Joel.
TOM: I'm Servo.

974
00:43:28,867 --> 00:43:30,467
CROW: And I'm Crow.
- No, I'm a reporter.

975
00:43:30,567 --> 00:43:32,900
TOM: I'm a robot.
JOEL: I'm a human being.

976
00:43:33,000 --> 00:43:34,300
- You're his doctor, I presume?

977
00:43:34,400 --> 00:43:36,967
- Not exactly.
Lorenz is a doctor himself

978
00:43:37,067 --> 00:43:38,400
but he has no license
to practice.

979
00:43:38,500 --> 00:43:40,033
JOEL: Making him a quack.
- ...working with him.

980
00:43:40,133 --> 00:43:42,067
We're trying to find
a cure for his wife.

981
00:43:42,167 --> 00:43:43,400
- Oh, I see.

982
00:43:44,433 --> 00:43:46,867
What type of people
are the Lorenzs?

983
00:43:46,967 --> 00:43:49,433
JOEL: Human.
- ...find them very interesting.

984
00:43:49,533 --> 00:43:52,467
Professor Lorenz is a man
of unusual accomplishment.

985
00:43:52,567 --> 00:43:55,000
But his wife
is rather peculiar.

986
00:43:55,100 --> 00:43:57,667
I expect you'll find them both
a bit eccentric.

987
00:43:59,567 --> 00:44:00,500
JOEL:
I like that.

988
00:44:00,600 --> 00:44:02,900
Eccentric.
Think we'll see Stanley?

989
00:44:04,000 --> 00:44:05,267
TOM:
No, I think-- no.

990
00:44:05,367 --> 00:44:07,067
It's Janie,
the garage door opener.

991
00:44:07,167 --> 00:44:09,367
[JOEL LAUGHS]

992
00:44:09,467 --> 00:44:11,167
CROW:
He hates those jokes.

993
00:44:13,000 --> 00:44:14,000
TOM:
He started it.

994
00:44:14,100 --> 00:44:15,000
CROW:
He was frightened

995
00:44:15,100 --> 00:44:16,467
by a garage door
as a child.

996
00:44:18,833 --> 00:44:21,100
JOEL:
You know, he used to play
at the Ice Capades.

997
00:44:24,167 --> 00:44:25,767
CROW:
Why, nobody eats the--

998
00:44:25,867 --> 00:44:28,067
oh, I didn't see it
down there.

999
00:44:32,267 --> 00:44:34,033
- Toby,
tell your master I'm here.

1000
00:44:36,133 --> 00:44:37,833
TOM:
May I take your skirt?

1001
00:44:37,933 --> 00:44:39,867
JOEL:
Is it just me,
or is he really small?

1002
00:44:39,967 --> 00:44:41,167
CROW:
It's called foreshortening.

1003
00:44:41,267 --> 00:44:42,533
- ...isn't he?

1004
00:44:42,633 --> 00:44:44,200
- I told you
they were eccentric.

1005
00:44:44,300 --> 00:44:46,433
- I'm inclined
to believe you.

1006
00:44:51,933 --> 00:44:52,967
TOM:
Excuse me.

1007
00:44:53,067 --> 00:44:54,867
Hey, you up there.

1008
00:44:54,967 --> 00:44:56,733
You, up there!

1009
00:44:56,833 --> 00:44:58,500
- Get out, you gargoyle.

1010
00:44:58,600 --> 00:45:00,700
Get out!

1011
00:45:00,800 --> 00:45:03,633
CROW:
You're no prize yourself, babe.

1012
00:45:03,733 --> 00:45:07,067
- Good evening, Dr. Foster.
Come right in.

1013
00:45:07,167 --> 00:45:09,833
- Good evening,
Professor Lorenz, Countess.

1014
00:45:09,933 --> 00:45:11,533
May I present
Miss Patricia Hunter.

1015
00:45:11,633 --> 00:45:13,067
- How do you do.

1016
00:45:13,167 --> 00:45:14,667
- We have been expecting you.

1017
00:45:14,767 --> 00:45:16,300
- So, you found my bag
in your truck.

1018
00:45:16,400 --> 00:45:18,667
- Yes. There it is.

1019
00:45:18,767 --> 00:45:20,000
- Miss Hunter's
a newspaper woman, and...

1020
00:45:20,100 --> 00:45:22,167
- I came here hoping you might
grant me an interview.

1021
00:45:22,267 --> 00:45:24,367
- An interview?
About what?

1022
00:45:24,467 --> 00:45:26,067
- No one asked you
to come here.

1023
00:45:26,167 --> 00:45:27,133
You are not welcome!

1024
00:45:27,233 --> 00:45:28,600
ALL:
Nice tag!

1025
00:45:28,700 --> 00:45:30,300
- ...my dear.

1026
00:45:30,400 --> 00:45:32,467
I'm sure Miss Hunter
meant no harm.

1027
00:45:32,567 --> 00:45:34,467
Please forgive my wife,
she's not well.

1028
00:45:34,567 --> 00:45:36,533
Come, you'd better retire.

1029
00:45:37,767 --> 00:45:40,200
CROW:
Good punch for a southpaw.

1030
00:45:40,300 --> 00:45:43,067
- So, that's what you call
being eccentric.

1031
00:45:43,167 --> 00:45:44,767
Well, I have
another name for it.

1032
00:45:44,867 --> 00:45:46,333
CROW:
I call it "bitchy."

1033
00:45:47,900 --> 00:45:50,067
- I do not like that girl.

1034
00:45:50,167 --> 00:45:52,500
She's here for no good.

1035
00:45:52,600 --> 00:45:53,667
- We have nothing to fear.

1036
00:45:53,767 --> 00:45:55,333
JOEL:
But fear itself.

1037
00:45:56,933 --> 00:45:59,500
TOM:
♪ The weather started
Getting rough ♪

1038
00:45:59,600 --> 00:46:01,867
- I'm sorry, Professor,
if we're intruding.

1039
00:46:01,967 --> 00:46:03,767
- Not at all, Dr. Foster.

1040
00:46:03,867 --> 00:46:05,733
This is our evening for work.

1041
00:46:06,967 --> 00:46:08,133
Please, be seated.

1042
00:46:08,233 --> 00:46:09,133
- Thank you.

1043
00:46:09,233 --> 00:46:11,600
TOM:
My wife tends to
slap everybody.

1044
00:46:11,700 --> 00:46:13,533
- What do you wish
to interview me about.

1045
00:46:13,633 --> 00:46:15,600
- Orchids.
- Orchids?

1046
00:46:15,700 --> 00:46:17,467
- Yes, I understand
that you're an authority

1047
00:46:17,567 --> 00:46:19,400
on the subject.

1048
00:46:19,500 --> 00:46:21,433
- They were once my hobby,

1049
00:46:21,533 --> 00:46:24,233
but I haven't grown any
for several years.

1050
00:46:25,800 --> 00:46:28,267
Which species
are you interested in?

1051
00:46:28,367 --> 00:46:30,967
- A very rare one,
of the Stanhope type.

1052
00:46:31,067 --> 00:46:32,133
A horticulturist told me

1053
00:46:32,233 --> 00:46:34,367
that you're the original
hybridizer of it.

1054
00:46:34,467 --> 00:46:36,667
CROW:
Nobody calls me that and lives.

1055
00:46:36,767 --> 00:46:38,600
- Yes, I have one in my bag.

1056
00:46:38,700 --> 00:46:39,700
I'll show it to you.

1057
00:46:39,800 --> 00:46:41,033
TOM:
I got it off a dead bride.

1058
00:46:41,133 --> 00:46:42,867
- Where did you get it?

1059
00:46:42,967 --> 00:46:44,500
- Oh, um...

1060
00:46:44,600 --> 00:46:46,100
a friend of mine
gave it to me.

1061
00:46:46,200 --> 00:46:48,567
You see, I'm doing an article
on orchids for my newspaper,

1062
00:46:48,667 --> 00:46:50,467
and I thought that you'd be
willing to supply me

1063
00:46:50,567 --> 00:46:51,800
with some special data.

1064
00:46:51,900 --> 00:46:54,633
- I'm sorry, I can't spare
the time tonight, Miss Hunter.

1065
00:46:54,733 --> 00:46:57,033
You see, I have very important
work to do with Dr. Foster.

1066
00:46:57,133 --> 00:46:59,167
CROW: The real doctor.
- I understand.

1067
00:46:59,267 --> 00:47:00,533
- If your interview
can wait until morning,

1068
00:47:00,633 --> 00:47:02,167
I'll drive you
back here then.

1069
00:47:02,267 --> 00:47:03,833
If that's agreeable
with Professor Lorenz.

1070
00:47:03,933 --> 00:47:05,233
- It's quite agreeable
but I would suggest

1071
00:47:05,333 --> 00:47:07,033
that you both stay here
as my guests.

1072
00:47:07,133 --> 00:47:09,667
- Thank you very much,
but I prefer going to a hotel.

1073
00:47:09,767 --> 00:47:11,433
- Just as you wish.

1074
00:47:11,533 --> 00:47:12,567
CROW:
Why, I happen to have a hotel

1075
00:47:12,667 --> 00:47:14,600
right behind this curtain.

1076
00:47:17,967 --> 00:47:20,500
- I think it would be foolish
to go out in such storm.

1077
00:47:20,600 --> 00:47:21,833
- The professor is right,
Miss Hunter.

1078
00:47:21,933 --> 00:47:23,600
Perhaps we had better remain.

1079
00:47:23,700 --> 00:47:24,600
However, if you insist,

1080
00:47:24,700 --> 00:47:26,400
I'll gladly drive you back
to the village.

1081
00:47:26,500 --> 00:47:27,733
- I'm afraid that would be
an imposition

1082
00:47:27,833 --> 00:47:29,533
since you've
so much work to do.

1083
00:47:29,633 --> 00:47:31,733
TOM: Yes, it would.
- Thank you, I'll stay.

1084
00:47:31,833 --> 00:47:33,400
JOEL:
But I don't like it.

1085
00:47:36,200 --> 00:47:37,867
- Toby will show you
to your rooms.

1086
00:47:37,967 --> 00:47:38,867
TOM:
You'll be sleeping

1087
00:47:38,967 --> 00:47:40,300
with the monkey-boy tonight.

1088
00:47:41,533 --> 00:47:43,767
- Toby, take up
Miss Hunter's bags.

1089
00:47:46,300 --> 00:47:47,833
JOEL:
He's a bellboy too.

1090
00:47:49,733 --> 00:47:50,700
- Goodnight.

1091
00:47:50,800 --> 00:47:52,733
- Goodnight, I'll meet you
in the study soon.

1092
00:47:52,833 --> 00:47:53,833
- Very well.

1093
00:47:56,633 --> 00:47:59,500
CROW:
These kooks are
coming out of the woodwork.

1094
00:48:04,233 --> 00:48:06,600
- Why did you ask her
to spend the night here?

1095
00:48:06,700 --> 00:48:07,600
JOEL [IMITATES LUGOSI]:
She's got legs

1096
00:48:07,700 --> 00:48:09,333
and she knows
how to use them.

1097
00:48:09,433 --> 00:48:12,667
- Very special reason.

1098
00:48:13,933 --> 00:48:15,433
TOM:
He's been injecting her
with young and pretty,

1099
00:48:15,533 --> 00:48:18,433
now he should start injecting
her with smart and nice.

1100
00:48:26,700 --> 00:48:29,200
- Thank you.
JOEL: Is it appropriate to tip?

1101
00:48:30,200 --> 00:48:31,767
CROW:
Long as you write him again.

1102
00:48:33,133 --> 00:48:35,267
TOM:
Think if you tipped him,
he'd fall right over.

1103
00:48:35,367 --> 00:48:39,100
- I guess you
sleep very good, maybe.

1104
00:48:39,200 --> 00:48:40,100
[SINISTER LAUGH]

1105
00:48:40,200 --> 00:48:41,800
JOEL: [CHUCKLES]
He thinks that's funny?

1106
00:48:41,900 --> 00:48:43,000
- Few times I've stayed
overnight here,

1107
00:48:43,100 --> 00:48:44,600
I've slept unusually well.

1108
00:48:44,700 --> 00:48:48,067
- Must be wonderful
to have nerves like that.

1109
00:48:48,167 --> 00:48:50,433
Tell me, just how well
do you know these people.

1110
00:48:50,533 --> 00:48:52,433
- Why actually,
not very well.

1111
00:48:52,533 --> 00:48:54,033
I've only recently moved
into the village.

1112
00:48:54,133 --> 00:48:55,033
You know, a young doctor

1113
00:48:55,133 --> 00:48:56,167
trying to
establish a practice.

1114
00:48:56,267 --> 00:48:57,567
CROW:
And a mustache.

1115
00:48:59,700 --> 00:49:02,000
- Well, I must be
keeping you from your work.

1116
00:49:02,100 --> 00:49:03,900
Goodnight, doctor.
- Goodnight.

1117
00:49:06,367 --> 00:49:07,800
TOM:
Oops, wrong scene.

1118
00:49:09,967 --> 00:49:11,033
JOEL:
I think she's looking

1119
00:49:11,133 --> 00:49:13,233
for the other two
Andrew Sisters.

1120
00:49:14,833 --> 00:49:16,267
CROW:
Patty and Maxene?

1121
00:49:19,733 --> 00:49:21,000
- What do you want?

1122
00:49:21,100 --> 00:49:23,667
- Yes, you are beautiful.

1123
00:49:23,767 --> 00:49:25,000
- How did you get in here?

1124
00:49:25,100 --> 00:49:26,900
- So young.

1125
00:49:27,000 --> 00:49:28,967
Such lovely skin.

1126
00:49:29,067 --> 00:49:30,333
TOM:
You'd look good
rotating on the spit.

1127
00:49:30,433 --> 00:49:33,700
- Sometime you too
will be a bride.

1128
00:49:33,800 --> 00:49:34,933
CROW:
Fresh.

1129
00:49:40,900 --> 00:49:42,933
Hey,
I was gonna make espresso.

1130
00:49:43,033 --> 00:49:44,167
Where'd you go?

1131
00:49:50,067 --> 00:49:53,000
TOM:
People rarely walk out
on international coffees.

1132
00:50:01,100 --> 00:50:03,367
JOEL:
He's taken all her toiletries.

1133
00:50:04,400 --> 00:50:06,333
Oh, that little guy,

1134
00:50:06,433 --> 00:50:09,500
he probably just wants
some attention.

1135
00:50:09,600 --> 00:50:12,433
He really likes her so...

1136
00:50:12,533 --> 00:50:15,867
Maybe he's out in the yard
playing on a swing set.

1137
00:50:15,967 --> 00:50:18,233
I can't confront him, though.

1138
00:50:18,333 --> 00:50:21,300
Yes, yes,
let it be known I love him.

1139
00:50:27,333 --> 00:50:30,367
Doc, Dopey, Sneezy.

1140
00:50:45,267 --> 00:50:46,167
TOM:
This is much better

1141
00:50:46,267 --> 00:50:48,667
than my own passage
through the sock drawer.

1142
00:51:06,300 --> 00:51:07,367
CROW:
While the reporter sleeps,

1143
00:51:07,467 --> 00:51:10,033
a pasty film covers her mouth.

1144
00:51:13,500 --> 00:51:15,533
JOEL [IMITATES LUGOSI]:
I just love to watch her sleep.

1145
00:51:15,633 --> 00:51:18,033
Look at how pretty
her hair looks.

1146
00:51:18,133 --> 00:51:20,267
Her pajamas are darling.

1147
00:51:20,367 --> 00:51:21,633
Sure, I love my wife,

1148
00:51:21,733 --> 00:51:24,633
but the upkeep on her
is unbelievable.

1149
00:51:24,733 --> 00:51:26,933
It's outrageous,
seven corpses a week.

1150
00:51:27,033 --> 00:51:28,900
If only she could cut back.

1151
00:51:29,000 --> 00:51:31,267
Now I'll walk backwards.

1152
00:51:36,067 --> 00:51:36,967
Yes.

1153
00:51:37,067 --> 00:51:37,967
TOM:
I've gotta go back here

1154
00:51:38,067 --> 00:51:39,767
and talk to the lion
and the witch.

1155
00:51:42,333 --> 00:51:45,733
CROW:
She awakes with the worst
breath of the day.

1156
00:51:47,533 --> 00:51:49,033
JOEL:
But it's his breath.

1157
00:51:50,033 --> 00:51:51,033
CROW:
Ew.

1158
00:51:57,500 --> 00:52:00,233
TOM:
Maybe I was just
imagining things.

1159
00:52:00,333 --> 00:52:03,400
[♪♪♪]

1160
00:52:12,500 --> 00:52:14,600
TOM:
Meanwhile,
back at the mutant heap,

1161
00:52:14,700 --> 00:52:16,400
Stanley gets ready
for another grueling day

1162
00:52:16,500 --> 00:52:18,200
at the asbestos factory.

1163
00:52:21,833 --> 00:52:23,767
- I know where you're going.

1164
00:52:23,867 --> 00:52:25,833
[GROANS]

1165
00:52:25,933 --> 00:52:28,267
CROW:
Hey, don't be short with me.

1166
00:52:30,133 --> 00:52:32,967
- Some day,
the master will catch you.

1167
00:52:33,067 --> 00:52:34,033
[GROANS]

1168
00:52:34,133 --> 00:52:35,267
- Then you'll be sorry.

1169
00:52:35,367 --> 00:52:36,267
TOM:
What's he gonna do,

1170
00:52:36,367 --> 00:52:38,167
straighten his spine out?

1171
00:52:38,267 --> 00:52:39,500
JOEL:
Boy, somebody sure woke up

1172
00:52:39,600 --> 00:52:42,433
on the wrong side
of the slab this morning.

1173
00:52:43,667 --> 00:52:44,967
Dig my shadow?

1174
00:52:45,067 --> 00:52:46,167
[TOM GIGGLES]

1175
00:52:51,300 --> 00:52:55,300
JOEL [IMITATES LUGOSI]:
No, this man
is a different story.

1176
00:52:55,400 --> 00:52:58,133
No, his hair is all wrong.

1177
00:52:58,233 --> 00:53:01,200
Maybe I get rid
of the mustache definitely.

1178
00:53:01,300 --> 00:53:04,400
It's a crime to hide
those classic features.

1179
00:53:04,500 --> 00:53:06,733
He sleeps so peacefully.

1180
00:53:06,833 --> 00:53:09,233
It's a shame to hide him.

1181
00:53:09,333 --> 00:53:11,200
I must walk backwards.

1182
00:53:17,100 --> 00:53:19,167
TOM:
Night of the Living Shadows.

1183
00:53:20,433 --> 00:53:22,133
CROW:
Hey, I brought you
some cheese bread

1184
00:53:22,233 --> 00:53:26,933
and a little glass of milk.

1185
00:53:27,033 --> 00:53:27,933
JOEL:
Now, if I had my way,

1186
00:53:28,033 --> 00:53:29,967
I'd do her hair
completely differently.

1187
00:53:30,067 --> 00:53:31,133
I'd toss her hair back

1188
00:53:31,233 --> 00:53:33,700
and rope it off
with a little chaparral.

1189
00:53:33,800 --> 00:53:34,867
It'd be just so lovely.

1190
00:53:34,967 --> 00:53:37,033
See, she's lost
a lot of protein,

1191
00:53:37,133 --> 00:53:39,067
I'd give her
a kind of a protein wash

1192
00:53:39,167 --> 00:53:41,200
and then a rinse
and then maybe we'd...

1193
00:53:41,300 --> 00:53:44,000
[SCREAMS]

1194
00:53:44,100 --> 00:53:45,867
CROW:
I don't think she wants a wash.

1195
00:53:48,567 --> 00:53:49,900
TOM:
Make sure you get
all those clothes on.

1196
00:53:50,000 --> 00:53:50,900
Yeah, get everything.

1197
00:53:51,000 --> 00:53:52,500
Get the shoes,
the housecoat...

1198
00:53:54,133 --> 00:53:56,833
CROW:
You might say that she's
dressing for distress.

1199
00:53:58,133 --> 00:53:59,133
TOM:
Smartly done,

1200
00:53:59,233 --> 00:54:01,600
but she just does not know
how to accessorize.

1201
00:54:03,800 --> 00:54:05,800
- Dr. Foster?
Dr. Foster?

1202
00:54:05,900 --> 00:54:07,100
CROW:
Dr. Denton?

1203
00:54:11,467 --> 00:54:13,500
- Dr. Foster?
Dr. Foster?

1204
00:54:13,600 --> 00:54:15,600
TOM:
Dr. Scholls? Dr. Pepper?

1205
00:54:17,567 --> 00:54:18,733
CROW:
Kinky.

1206
00:54:18,833 --> 00:54:20,167
JOEL:
Oh, they look so good.

1207
00:54:20,267 --> 00:54:23,533
So peaceful.
They never looked that good.

1208
00:54:25,367 --> 00:54:27,067
- Dr. Foster.
- What's wrong?

1209
00:54:27,167 --> 00:54:28,333
- I just saw someone
in my room.

1210
00:54:28,433 --> 00:54:30,133
A horrible looking creature.

1211
00:54:30,233 --> 00:54:31,333
CROW:
Aw, that's just Stanley,

1212
00:54:31,433 --> 00:54:33,967
the gnarled but lovable
garage door opener.

1213
00:54:37,067 --> 00:54:39,067
- He disappeared back there.

1214
00:54:52,767 --> 00:54:54,500
JOEL:
Oh, you've got some
nice outfits in here.

1215
00:54:54,600 --> 00:54:55,767
Love that Teddy.

1216
00:54:57,133 --> 00:54:59,400
CROW: Teddy bear?
- Well, he's not here now.

1217
00:54:59,500 --> 00:55:02,167
Where did you see him?
- Leaning over my bed.

1218
00:55:02,267 --> 00:55:03,433
- 'Course, you might
have been dreaming.

1219
00:55:03,533 --> 00:55:05,100
- I'm positive I wasn't.

1220
00:55:05,200 --> 00:55:06,300
Earlier in the evening,

1221
00:55:06,400 --> 00:55:08,967
Mrs. Lorenz suddenly appeared
and then disappeared.

1222
00:55:09,067 --> 00:55:11,400
Do you suppose there's
another entrance to this room?

1223
00:55:11,500 --> 00:55:12,967
- Oh, I doubt it.

1224
00:55:13,067 --> 00:55:14,467
Why don't you try
to go back to sleep.

1225
00:55:14,567 --> 00:55:16,100
No one's going to harm you.

1226
00:55:16,200 --> 00:55:17,767
I'm sure it was just
a nightmare.

1227
00:55:17,867 --> 00:55:19,800
- I think this whole place
is a nightmare.

1228
00:55:19,900 --> 00:55:21,133
ALL:
So do we.

1229
