1468
01:07:01,267 --> 01:07:03,000
- Well, that'll have to wait
until some other time.

1469
01:07:03,100 --> 01:07:05,000
I just remembered
an appointment at the office,

1470
01:07:05,100 --> 01:07:08,233
so I've got to catch
the next train.

1471
01:07:08,333 --> 01:07:09,533
If you'll excuse me, Countess?

1472
01:07:09,633 --> 01:07:11,467
- Yes, I will excuse you.

1473
01:07:11,567 --> 01:07:12,800
JOEL:
And then I'll slap you.

1474
01:07:12,900 --> 01:07:15,900
- Professor, do you also make
a hobby of collecting coffins?

1475
01:07:16,000 --> 01:07:18,033
- Why yes,
in a manner of speaking.

1476
01:07:18,133 --> 01:07:21,233
I find a coffin much more
comfortable than a bed.

1477
01:07:21,333 --> 01:07:22,233
CROW [IMITATES LUGOSI]:
It's a water coffin.

1478
01:07:22,333 --> 01:07:24,267
- Many people do so, my dear.

1479
01:07:24,367 --> 01:07:26,967
Is it so strange
that I accept one

1480
01:07:27,067 --> 01:07:29,300
while waiting
for eternal rest?

1481
01:07:29,400 --> 01:07:31,267
- No, I suppose not.

1482
01:07:31,367 --> 01:07:33,267
Well, thank you
for your hospitality.

1483
01:07:33,367 --> 01:07:35,133
Goodbye.

1484
01:07:35,233 --> 01:07:36,867
TOM:
Wait, it is strange.

1485
01:07:36,967 --> 01:07:39,200
- I trust
we will meet again.

1486
01:07:39,300 --> 01:07:41,933
It has been so nice
meeting you.

1487
01:07:42,033 --> 01:07:43,467
JOEL:
What a sweet couple.

1488
01:07:44,633 --> 01:07:45,633
CROW:
Kooky.

1489
01:07:48,433 --> 01:07:50,267
- Don't tell me
I'm still dreaming.

1490
01:07:50,367 --> 01:07:51,267
- Well, what do you mean?

1491
01:07:51,367 --> 01:07:53,900
- That gravedigger,
he's real, isn't he?

1492
01:07:54,000 --> 01:07:55,467
JOEL:
What gravedigger?

1493
01:07:57,633 --> 01:07:58,833
- Oh, he's real alright.

1494
01:07:58,933 --> 01:08:00,567
But I hardly think
he's a gravedigger.

1495
01:08:00,667 --> 01:08:02,633
More likely laying
the foundation for a house.

1496
01:08:02,733 --> 01:08:04,867
- You're a trusting soul,
aren't you, Doctor?

1497
01:08:04,967 --> 01:08:05,867
- Is that a crime?

1498
01:08:05,967 --> 01:08:08,467
- No, on the contrary.
I envy you.

1499
01:08:08,567 --> 01:08:09,467
- Are you trying
to make me believe

1500
01:08:09,567 --> 01:08:10,967
you're one of those
hard boiled reporters

1501
01:08:11,067 --> 01:08:12,667
that we read about
or see in the movies?

1502
01:08:12,767 --> 01:08:14,100
- No, but working
on a newspaper,

1503
01:08:14,200 --> 01:08:16,800
one becomes cynical
and suspicious.

1504
01:08:16,900 --> 01:08:19,200
CROW:
Aw, she writes for Crappy Dick.

1505
01:08:34,667 --> 01:08:35,567
What a bucket of bolts.

1506
01:08:35,667 --> 01:08:36,933
- I've enjoyed meeting you,
Miss Hunter.

1507
01:08:37,033 --> 01:08:38,467
I hope to have the pleasure
of seeing you again.

1508
01:08:38,567 --> 01:08:40,233
- I have a hunch you will,
Doctor Foster.

1509
01:08:40,333 --> 01:08:41,233
TOM:
You have a hunch too?

1510
01:08:41,333 --> 01:08:43,000
I guess it's contagious.

1511
01:08:43,100 --> 01:08:44,900
- ...just why did you
come up here,

1512
01:08:45,000 --> 01:08:46,133
or is it a secret?

1513
01:08:46,233 --> 01:08:50,467
- It is, but somehow I feel
that I can trust you.

1514
01:08:50,567 --> 01:08:51,600
- You can.

1515
01:08:51,700 --> 01:08:53,900
- You've heard of the case
of the vanishing brides?

1516
01:08:54,000 --> 01:08:55,233
- Why, surely.

1517
01:08:55,333 --> 01:08:57,367
- I think I have a clue.

1518
01:08:57,467 --> 01:08:58,567
- You don't mean
that you suspect...

1519
01:08:58,667 --> 01:09:00,633
- Yes, I do.

1520
01:09:00,733 --> 01:09:02,633
I wanna show you something.

1521
01:09:04,000 --> 01:09:05,000
What are these?

1522
01:09:05,100 --> 01:09:06,500
- Why, they're orchids,
of course.

1523
01:09:06,600 --> 01:09:08,000
- You bet they're orchids.

1524
01:09:08,100 --> 01:09:10,200
And they didn't grow
in my dreams.

1525
01:09:10,300 --> 01:09:12,833
They're exactly likely the one
that disappeared from my bag.

1526
01:09:12,933 --> 01:09:14,367
TOM:
They grew in... [indistinct].

1527
01:09:14,467 --> 01:09:16,900
- I found them on the floor
beside my bed this morning.

1528
01:09:17,000 --> 01:09:18,567
The person who came
into my room last night

1529
01:09:18,667 --> 01:09:20,200
must've brought these with him.

1530
01:09:20,300 --> 01:09:22,200
That seems like
a logical explanation.

1531
01:09:22,300 --> 01:09:23,833
- There's
no question about it.

1532
01:09:23,933 --> 01:09:25,967
Honestly, Doctor,
don't you remember

1533
01:09:26,067 --> 01:09:28,133
meeting me in the hallway
last night.

1534
01:09:28,233 --> 01:09:29,633
- No, I don't.

1535
01:09:29,733 --> 01:09:32,967
And if I did I--
I must've been asleep.

1536
01:09:33,067 --> 01:09:35,733
- Either that,
or you were hypnotized.

1537
01:09:35,833 --> 01:09:37,900
- Yes, that's quite possible.

1538
01:09:38,000 --> 01:09:40,167
CROW:
He may still be.

1539
01:09:40,267 --> 01:09:41,800
TOM:
[indistinct].

1540
01:09:41,900 --> 01:09:42,800
JOEL:
Looks like a train,

1541
01:09:42,900 --> 01:09:45,067
but he'll need proof.

1542
01:09:45,167 --> 01:09:46,800
- Well, watch your step.

1543
01:09:46,900 --> 01:09:48,733
And if you discover
anything, call me.

1544
01:09:48,833 --> 01:09:50,233
- That's a promise.
- Goodbye.

1545
01:09:50,333 --> 01:09:51,833
- Not goodbye, au revoir.

1546
01:09:51,933 --> 01:09:53,500
CROW:
No, not au revoir, ciao.

1547
01:09:53,600 --> 01:09:54,800
No, not ciao, aloha.

1548
01:09:54,900 --> 01:09:56,600
No, not aloha, aufiderzein!

1549
01:09:56,700 --> 01:09:58,467
- Alright,
print the story on page one.

1550
01:09:58,567 --> 01:09:59,867
[JOEL MUFFLED SPEECH]

1551
01:09:59,967 --> 01:10:01,067
- ...fingers crossed
that this paper

1552
01:10:01,167 --> 01:10:02,667
isn't sued for libel
by some of these phony suspects

1553
01:10:02,767 --> 01:10:03,767
that the police
are bringing in.

1554
01:10:03,867 --> 01:10:05,100
[JOEL MUFFLED SPEECH]

1555
01:10:05,200 --> 01:10:06,633
...get me a [INDISTINCT] outta
the guy they just brought in.

1556
01:10:06,733 --> 01:10:09,500
- I don't want photographs.
I want real clues.

1557
01:10:09,600 --> 01:10:11,400
If the DA's office
can't pick up the madman

1558
01:10:11,500 --> 01:10:12,967
who's stealing the bodies
of these dead girls,

1559
01:10:13,067 --> 01:10:14,167
maybe we can.

1560
01:10:14,267 --> 01:10:16,233
- Okay, I'll throw it
in the wastebasket.

1561
01:10:16,333 --> 01:10:18,433
JOEL: The script?
- No, have it developed.

1562
01:10:18,533 --> 01:10:19,967
[indistinct]
Where's Lefty?

1563
01:10:20,067 --> 01:10:21,300
- Over at Central Station.

1564
01:10:21,400 --> 01:10:22,300
- The only thing
he gets over there

1565
01:10:22,400 --> 01:10:23,333
is a game of gin rummy.

1566
01:10:23,433 --> 01:10:24,467
Tell him to get back here.

1567
01:10:24,567 --> 01:10:25,467
- Well, I got something.

1568
01:10:25,567 --> 01:10:27,300
- I'll say you have,
you're fired.

1569
01:10:27,400 --> 01:10:28,967
- What do you mean?
You can't do that to me.

1570
01:10:29,067 --> 01:10:30,100
- Listen, just because

1571
01:10:30,200 --> 01:10:31,467
the bodies of dead girls
disappear

1572
01:10:31,567 --> 01:10:33,500
is no reason
you can do a vanishing act.

1573
01:10:33,600 --> 01:10:34,767
- But you gave me
the assignment.

1574
01:10:34,867 --> 01:10:35,767
Yeah, I suppose I did.

1575
01:10:35,867 --> 01:10:36,933
Alright,
where have you been?

1576
01:10:37,033 --> 01:10:38,133
What've you uncovered?

1577
01:10:38,233 --> 01:10:39,567
- Well, hey, not so fast.

1578
01:10:39,667 --> 01:10:41,533
Listen, Mr. Keenan,
last night I went through

1579
01:10:41,633 --> 01:10:43,367
the most gruesome
experience of my life.

1580
01:10:43,467 --> 01:10:44,367
- You should talk.

1581
01:10:44,467 --> 01:10:46,033
All of my life
has been a nightmare.

1582
01:10:46,133 --> 01:10:48,967
- Well, you can rest easier now,
because I've seen plenty.

1583
01:10:49,067 --> 01:10:50,667
If I can only prove
I wasn't dreaming.

1584
01:10:50,767 --> 01:10:52,100
- Dreaming?

1585
01:10:52,200 --> 01:10:54,000
- I had a dream
last night myself.

1586
01:10:54,100 --> 01:10:56,433
I dreamed you gave me that raise
you been promising me.

1587
01:10:56,533 --> 01:10:58,133
TOM:
Thank you, Shecky.

1588
01:10:58,233 --> 01:11:00,067
- ...go on with
your dream story.

1589
01:11:00,167 --> 01:11:01,800
- Well, there's a certain
Professor Lorenz

1590
01:11:01,900 --> 01:11:03,133
that lives upstate.

1591
01:11:03,233 --> 01:11:04,233
He knows all about orchids

1592
01:11:04,333 --> 01:11:06,400
but insists that he doesn't
grow them anymore.

1593
01:11:06,500 --> 01:11:08,967
Yet, I found these
in my bedroom this morning.

1594
01:11:10,333 --> 01:11:11,867
- Listen [indistinct]
you didn't go off someplace

1595
01:11:11,967 --> 01:11:12,867
and get married, did you?

1596
01:11:12,967 --> 01:11:14,033
- No, I didn't.

1597
01:11:14,133 --> 01:11:17,233
But I saw the bodies
of those other missing brides.

1598
01:11:17,333 --> 01:11:19,367
- Alright.
Now take it easy.

1599
01:11:19,467 --> 01:11:22,633
Stop breathing hard, Pat,
and tell me all about it.

1600
01:11:22,733 --> 01:11:24,767
Did you really see
any bodies?

1601
01:11:24,867 --> 01:11:26,633
- Yes, and no.

1602
01:11:26,733 --> 01:11:28,867
- I see.
Just a bit of ectoplasm.

1603
01:11:28,967 --> 01:11:30,467
JOEL:
Yeah, on the front
of your jacket.

1604
01:11:30,567 --> 01:11:33,000
- ...only...
Oh, I can't explain it.

1605
01:11:33,100 --> 01:11:35,100
- Yeah, you know something
the same way I am.

1606
01:11:35,200 --> 01:11:36,733
I see brides everywhere,

1607
01:11:36,833 --> 01:11:39,067
hear wedding bells
ringing in my ears.

1608
01:11:39,167 --> 01:11:40,633
- And I saw a dead man too.

1609
01:11:40,733 --> 01:11:42,933
ALL:
And his hair was perfect!

1610
01:11:43,033 --> 01:11:45,400
- Very well.

1611
01:11:45,500 --> 01:11:46,533
JOEL:
Hubba, hubba, what a suit.

1612
01:11:46,633 --> 01:11:48,233
Let me clean that off.
- ...I'm sorry.

1613
01:11:48,333 --> 01:11:50,033
She's busy,
she's with Mr. Keenan.

1614
01:11:50,133 --> 01:11:51,200
- It's very important
that I see her.

1615
01:11:51,300 --> 01:11:52,200
I'm Dr. Foster.

1616
01:11:52,300 --> 01:11:54,033
I wonder if you'd mind
announcing me.

1617
01:11:54,133 --> 01:11:55,933
TOM:
And now, right here
in our very office,

1618
01:11:56,033 --> 01:11:59,233
a very talented young Doctor,
please welcome...

1619
01:11:59,333 --> 01:12:00,767
- ...insists on seeing
Miss Hunter.

1620
01:12:00,867 --> 01:12:02,900
- Tell him he can have
all of her time from now on.

1621
01:12:03,000 --> 01:12:05,000
- That's the Dr. Foster
I was telling you about.

1622
01:12:05,100 --> 01:12:06,633
Maybe he has more evidence.

1623
01:12:07,700 --> 01:12:09,933
- Alright,
send the saw bones in.

1624
01:12:10,033 --> 01:12:11,267
- Hey look,
if you don't mind,

1625
01:12:11,367 --> 01:12:13,900
I'm going out
for a nervous breakdown.

1626
01:12:14,000 --> 01:12:15,500
TOM:
Funny!

1627
01:12:15,600 --> 01:12:17,567
- Well Doctor,
you almost beat me here.

1628
01:12:17,667 --> 01:12:18,667
- Yes, I drove down.

1629
01:12:18,767 --> 01:12:20,600
- Dr. Foster,
Mr. Keenan, my editor.

1630
01:12:20,700 --> 01:12:21,700
- Very nice to meet you,
Mr. Keenan.

1631
01:12:21,800 --> 01:12:23,433
- How do you do?
This cub reporter of mine

1632
01:12:23,533 --> 01:12:24,800
has been telling me
a weird story

1633
01:12:24,900 --> 01:12:27,300
about a certain Dr. Lorenz
up in your neighborhood.

1634
01:12:27,400 --> 01:12:30,167
- It's weird alright,
but I'm confident it's true.

1635
01:12:30,267 --> 01:12:31,833
After you left,
I looked around the station

1636
01:12:31,933 --> 01:12:33,433
to find out if Lorenz
had any other coffins

1637
01:12:33,533 --> 01:12:34,633
assigned to him.

1638
01:12:34,733 --> 01:12:37,433
I found the consignment alright,
but instead of a coffin,

1639
01:12:37,533 --> 01:12:39,233
it was a box
filled with this stuff.

1640
01:12:39,333 --> 01:12:41,300
CROW:
Olive loaf.

1641
01:12:41,400 --> 01:12:43,500
- So what?
That's ordinary garden moss.

1642
01:12:43,600 --> 01:12:45,900
- It's moss, alright.
But not ordinary.

1643
01:12:46,000 --> 01:12:47,533
This is a special type
used by florists

1644
01:12:47,633 --> 01:12:49,767
and hybridizers
for growing orchids.

1645
01:12:49,867 --> 01:12:50,933
It's called ozmondin.

1646
01:12:51,033 --> 01:12:52,100
ALL [MUMBLING]:
Ozmondin?

1647
01:12:52,200 --> 01:12:53,567
- ...he does grow orchids
like these.

1648
01:12:53,667 --> 01:12:54,733
And the same kind were worn

1649
01:12:54,833 --> 01:12:56,833
by every one of the brides
that died.

1650
01:12:56,933 --> 01:12:58,467
- Well, maybe it is a clue.

1651
01:12:58,567 --> 01:13:00,667
But how do I know that the rest
isn't a cockeyed nightmare?

1652
01:13:00,767 --> 01:13:01,900
CROW:
Or a night-eyed cock-mare.

1653
01:13:02,000 --> 01:13:03,500
- ...you don't remember
talking to her.

1654
01:13:03,600 --> 01:13:04,500
- I could've talked to her

1655
01:13:04,600 --> 01:13:06,067
while in a somnambulistic
state,

1656
01:13:06,167 --> 01:13:07,733
under the influence
of hypnotism.

1657
01:13:07,833 --> 01:13:09,300
- Aw, now wait a minute.

1658
01:13:09,400 --> 01:13:11,333
Are you trying to tell me
that this Professor Lorenz

1659
01:13:11,433 --> 01:13:13,667
is a hypnotist
as well as a horticulturist?

1660
01:13:13,767 --> 01:13:15,167
JOEL:
And a ventriloquist.

1661
01:13:15,267 --> 01:13:16,667
- My short acquaintance with him
has convinced me

1662
01:13:16,767 --> 01:13:18,700
that he's a man
of unlimited talents.

1663
01:13:18,800 --> 01:13:20,633
He's strange, peculiar.

1664
01:13:20,733 --> 01:13:23,467
I've even suspected him
of being insane.

1665
01:13:23,567 --> 01:13:25,267
But the fact remains,
he's not only a doctor,

1666
01:13:25,367 --> 01:13:27,367
but a physicist and a scientist
of great ability.

1667
01:13:27,467 --> 01:13:28,500
TOM:
And a horticulturist.

1668
01:13:28,600 --> 01:13:29,767
- ...this hotheaded wife of his?

1669
01:13:29,867 --> 01:13:32,233
- I hesitate to bring her
into the case at all.

1670
01:13:32,333 --> 01:13:33,633
Professional ethics
forbid a doctor

1671
01:13:33,733 --> 01:13:35,433
talking
about his patients.

1672
01:13:35,533 --> 01:13:37,533
- Oh, forget about
your ethics, Doctor.

1673
01:13:37,633 --> 01:13:39,733
After all,
we're after a criminal.

1674
01:13:39,833 --> 01:13:41,833
How does this woman
fit into the picture?

1675
01:13:41,933 --> 01:13:43,000
Come on...

1676
01:13:43,100 --> 01:13:44,067
JOEL:
Just clearing him up
a little bit there.

1677
01:13:44,167 --> 01:13:45,267
- ...curious fact about her is

1678
01:13:45,367 --> 01:13:47,800
that although she has
the appearance of a young woman,

1679
01:13:47,900 --> 01:13:48,967
her heart and arteries
indicate

1680
01:13:49,067 --> 01:13:51,533
that she's at least
70 or 80 years old.

1681
01:13:51,633 --> 01:13:53,367
- Well, that doesn't
explain anything.

1682
01:13:53,467 --> 01:13:54,467
It certainly
doesn't explain why...

1683
01:13:54,567 --> 01:13:55,467
TOM:
It's tidy, now.

1684
01:13:55,567 --> 01:13:56,467
- ...should steal
these dead girls.

1685
01:13:56,567 --> 01:13:57,467
JOEL:
Nice and clean.

1686
01:13:57,567 --> 01:13:59,100
- It's possible
they may still be alive.

1687
01:13:59,200 --> 01:14:00,800
- Alive in a cataleptic state?

1688
01:14:00,900 --> 01:14:02,000
TOM:
Iowa?

1689
01:14:02,100 --> 01:14:04,300
- Alright, alright.
What are your other deductions?

1690
01:14:04,400 --> 01:14:05,700
- Simply this.

1691
01:14:05,800 --> 01:14:07,533
These young girls,
whether dead or alive,

1692
01:14:07,633 --> 01:14:09,833
are being used by Lorenz
in some manner,

1693
01:14:09,933 --> 01:14:10,833
as human guinea pigs

1694
01:14:10,933 --> 01:14:12,700
to sustain his wife
in a youthful state.

1695
01:14:12,800 --> 01:14:14,400
TOM: Bride juice.
- Oh, that's preposterous.

1696
01:14:14,500 --> 01:14:15,833
- It's nothing of the kind.

1697
01:14:15,933 --> 01:14:17,300
Scientists are finding out
every day

1698
01:14:17,400 --> 01:14:18,967
that glands and hormones
have a lot to do

1699
01:14:19,067 --> 01:14:20,400
with life and health.
JOEL: Yeah!

1700
01:14:20,500 --> 01:14:21,700
- The glands in our bodies
help determine

1701
01:14:21,800 --> 01:14:24,067
the condition of our teeth,
the texture of our hair...

1702
01:14:24,167 --> 01:14:26,167
CROW: The cut of our jib.
JOEL: Learning is fun.

1703
01:14:26,267 --> 01:14:28,567
- ...I wouldn't dare print
such a fantastic story.

1704
01:14:28,667 --> 01:14:30,200
- I bring you the biggest
story of the year

1705
01:14:30,300 --> 01:14:32,167
and a chance to get
this newspaper a real scoop,

1706
01:14:32,267 --> 01:14:33,833
and you call it fantastic?

1707
01:14:33,933 --> 01:14:36,267
- Alright, I'll put Doyle on it
to track it down.

1708
01:14:36,367 --> 01:14:37,700
- I'm afraid
you won't get very far.

1709
01:14:37,800 --> 01:14:39,300
Professor Lorenz
is a clever man.

1710
01:14:39,400 --> 01:14:41,200
Clever enough to withstand
any investigation

1711
01:14:41,300 --> 01:14:43,167
and destroy any evidence.

1712
01:14:43,267 --> 01:14:44,833
- Why not track the fella?

1713
01:14:44,933 --> 01:14:45,833
- How?

1714
01:14:45,933 --> 01:14:47,633
- Well, he steals
dead brides, doesn't he?

1715
01:14:47,733 --> 01:14:48,633
- Aw, now look, Pat.

1716
01:14:48,733 --> 01:14:50,067
Do you want to be
a June bride?

1717
01:14:50,167 --> 01:14:52,767
- Well, sure.
I mean, that is, no, not yet.

1718
01:14:52,867 --> 01:14:54,233
JOEL: Oh.
- Anyways, Lorenz knows me.

1719
01:14:54,333 --> 01:14:56,133
- Alright,
then stop talking nonsense.

1720
01:14:56,233 --> 01:14:57,833
This paper can't assume
the responsibility

1721
01:14:57,933 --> 01:14:59,767
of such a harebrained scheme.

1722
01:14:59,867 --> 01:15:01,500
- Oh, please, Mr. Keenan.

1723
01:15:01,600 --> 01:15:03,967
You can't pass up
an opportunity like this.

1724
01:15:04,067 --> 01:15:07,067
[♪♪♪]

1725
01:15:12,467 --> 01:15:15,967
CROW [WOMAN'S VOICE]:
Cigars, cigarettes, tiparillos?

1726
01:15:16,067 --> 01:15:17,333
- Give me a bourbon with soda.

1727
01:15:17,433 --> 01:15:18,500
- There she is.

1728
01:15:19,633 --> 01:15:21,167
- Cigarettes?

1729
01:15:21,267 --> 01:15:22,567
- Eh, she's cute.

1730
01:15:22,667 --> 01:15:24,933
CROW:
Well, if you're into
that sort of thing.

1731
01:15:25,033 --> 01:15:26,067
- Well, would you like to dance?

1732
01:15:26,167 --> 01:15:28,533
- Later. Business
before pleasure, you know.

1733
01:15:28,633 --> 01:15:29,533
JOEL:
Do you really think

1734
01:15:29,633 --> 01:15:31,300
it'd be a pleasure
to dance with him?

1735
01:15:31,400 --> 01:15:32,667
- Miss Woods, Dr. Foster.

1736
01:15:32,767 --> 01:15:34,433
- Good evening, Miss Woods.
- How do you do?

1737
01:15:34,533 --> 01:15:36,033
- I've something important
to tell you.

1738
01:15:36,133 --> 01:15:37,033
- I'm all ears.

1739
01:15:37,133 --> 01:15:38,367
- Let's go
to your dressing room.

1740
01:15:38,467 --> 01:15:39,933
- Okay.
- I won't be long.

1741
01:15:40,033 --> 01:15:41,033
- Alright.

1742
01:15:43,167 --> 01:15:44,667
JOEL:
Let's powder our noses.

1743
01:15:47,833 --> 01:15:50,000
- Peggy, I've landed
a good part for you.

1744
01:15:50,100 --> 01:15:51,633
A chance to prove your talent.

1745
01:15:51,733 --> 01:15:53,267
- Really? What is it,
a picture or a play?

1746
01:15:53,367 --> 01:15:54,333
JOEL:
Call girl.

1747
01:15:54,433 --> 01:15:55,967
- My paper's going to
stage a phony wedding

1748
01:15:56,067 --> 01:15:57,700
and we want you to be
the perfect June bride.

1749
01:15:57,800 --> 01:15:58,900
- Now wait just a minute...

1750
01:15:59,000 --> 01:16:01,000
- With bride's maids and flowers
and all the trimmings.

1751
01:16:01,100 --> 01:16:02,933
- What is it?
A circulation stunt?

1752
01:16:03,033 --> 01:16:04,000
- No.

1753
01:16:04,100 --> 01:16:05,433
You've heard of the case
of the vanishing brides.

1754
01:16:05,533 --> 01:16:06,600
- Sure.

1755
01:16:06,700 --> 01:16:07,967
- Well this is a plan we had
to track the person...

1756
01:16:08,067 --> 01:16:08,967
CROW:
What is it?

1757
01:16:09,067 --> 01:16:09,967
- ...who's been stealing
their bodies.

1758
01:16:10,067 --> 01:16:11,900
- Are you kidding?
- Certainly not.

1759
01:16:12,000 --> 01:16:13,500
The job will pay you plenty.

1760
01:16:13,600 --> 01:16:14,933
- How'll I collect
if I'm dead?

1761
01:16:15,033 --> 01:16:16,200
- You won't be dead.

1762
01:16:16,300 --> 01:16:19,000
All you have to be careful of
is not to smell any flowers.

1763
01:16:19,100 --> 01:16:20,600
- All I do is lie
six feet underground

1764
01:16:20,700 --> 01:16:21,600
and push them up, nix.

1765
01:16:21,700 --> 01:16:22,600
Count me out!

1766
01:16:22,700 --> 01:16:24,567
- Listen, Peggy.
I'm offering you a chance

1767
01:16:24,667 --> 01:16:26,067
to get your picture
on the front page

1768
01:16:26,167 --> 01:16:27,700
of every newspaper
in this country.

1769
01:16:27,800 --> 01:16:29,167
CROW:
And in the obituary.

1770
01:16:29,267 --> 01:16:31,200
- ...Broadway producers
and Hollywood studios

1771
01:16:31,300 --> 01:16:32,733
will be offering you contracts.

1772
01:16:32,833 --> 01:16:35,700
Why, it's the chance
you've been waiting for!

1773
01:16:35,800 --> 01:16:38,033
TOM:
That's really a load of hooey.

1774
01:16:39,067 --> 01:16:40,167
[GROANS]

1775
01:16:40,267 --> 01:16:42,333
CROW:
And there she is.

1776
01:16:42,433 --> 01:16:45,133
- She's the most unusual type.

1777
01:16:46,300 --> 01:16:48,367
JOEL [IMITATES LUGOSI]:
She's just my type, B-positive.

1778
01:16:48,467 --> 01:16:50,567
- She'd make a charming
addition to our family.

1779
01:16:51,800 --> 01:16:53,967
- June 20th.

1780
01:16:54,067 --> 01:16:56,033
- A date we shall not forget.

1781
01:16:56,133 --> 01:16:58,833
JOEL [IMITATES LUGOSI]:
A date that will live
in infamy!

1782
01:16:58,933 --> 01:17:01,033
[SINISTER LAUGH]

1783
01:17:01,133 --> 01:17:04,300
- Fagah, what are you doing
in this part of the house?

1784
01:17:04,400 --> 01:17:05,867
TOM:
What'd he call her?

1785
01:17:05,967 --> 01:17:09,667
- I keep hearing my son's voice
calling for me.

1786
01:17:09,767 --> 01:17:12,033
- Your son is better off
where he is.

1787
01:17:12,133 --> 01:17:13,200
Go back to your work.

1788
01:17:13,300 --> 01:17:15,867
- Master,
you shouldn't have done it.

1789
01:17:15,967 --> 01:17:17,800
He didn't deserve to die!

1790
01:17:17,900 --> 01:17:20,433
- Do what I say
and don't come up here again!

1791
01:17:20,533 --> 01:17:22,300
CROW:
Or it's community theatre
for you, babe.

1792
01:17:22,400 --> 01:17:23,867
- Yes, Master.

1793
01:17:23,967 --> 01:17:25,200
TOM:
Lorenz Leachman,
ladies and gentlemen,

1794
01:17:25,300 --> 01:17:28,267
give her a big hand.
Lorenz Leachman.

1795
01:17:31,733 --> 01:17:33,333
- Well, everything's all set.

1796
01:17:33,433 --> 01:17:34,533
She's young and pretty.

1797
01:17:34,633 --> 01:17:35,600
- Yeah.

1798
01:17:35,700 --> 01:17:37,000
What assurances have we
that this Lorenz guy

1799
01:17:37,100 --> 01:17:38,567
will show up
to make the snatch?

1800
01:17:38,667 --> 01:17:41,067
- And what happens if he does
kidnap this fake bride?

1801
01:17:41,167 --> 01:17:42,733
- Well, the private chapel
and the neighborhood

1802
01:17:42,833 --> 01:17:44,300
will be covered
with guards and well,

1803
01:17:44,400 --> 01:17:46,267
Lorenz hasn't
a chance to escape.

1804
01:17:46,367 --> 01:17:47,667
- Providing he shows up.

1805
01:17:47,767 --> 01:17:50,633
And if he doesn't,
I'm sucker and out 500 bucks.

1806
01:17:50,733 --> 01:17:51,833
- And if he does,

1807
01:17:51,933 --> 01:17:54,167
you have an exclusive story
with big juicy headlines.

1808
01:17:54,267 --> 01:17:56,400
JOEL:
Big, tender, sloppy headlines.

1809
01:17:56,500 --> 01:17:58,200
Slathered with sauce.

1810
01:17:58,300 --> 01:18:01,167
Chomp.
Juicy to a fault.

1811
01:18:03,233 --> 01:18:04,267
- Well, blushing bride,

1812
01:18:04,367 --> 01:18:06,267
here's where you go on
for your big scene.

1813
01:18:06,367 --> 01:18:08,400
- Or big flop.
My knees are shaking.

1814
01:18:08,500 --> 01:18:09,867
- There's nothing
to be afraid of.

1815
01:18:09,967 --> 01:18:12,233
Here's your bouquet
and the phony orchids.

1816
01:18:12,333 --> 01:18:13,933
Now don't forget
to put on your hat.

1817
01:18:14,033 --> 01:18:15,067
- Leave that to me.

1818
01:18:15,167 --> 01:18:16,633
You got plenty of
photographers out there?

1819
01:18:16,733 --> 01:18:18,733
- Certainly.
- And what about the cops?

1820
01:18:18,833 --> 01:18:20,133
- How many times
do I have to tell you?

1821
01:18:20,233 --> 01:18:21,733
Nothing's going
to happen to you.

1822
01:18:21,833 --> 01:18:23,000
- Oh, Miss Hunter.

1823
01:18:23,100 --> 01:18:24,233
- Yes?

1824
01:18:24,333 --> 01:18:25,533
JOEL: Doesn't smell weird to me.
- Uh, I wanted to ask you.

1825
01:18:25,633 --> 01:18:28,833
In this ceremony, shall we use
the word "cherish" or "obey?"

1826
01:18:28,933 --> 01:18:29,867
- You mean, for the bride?

1827
01:18:29,967 --> 01:18:31,400
- Yes.
- Well, just for this once,

1828
01:18:31,500 --> 01:18:34,233
let's put the old fashioned
word in, "obey."

1829
01:18:34,333 --> 01:18:36,100
- I've marked the words here.

1830
01:18:36,200 --> 01:18:40,000
Just read the ceremony
slowly, solemnly.

1831
01:18:40,100 --> 01:18:41,200
- Yes, I understand.

1832
01:18:41,300 --> 01:18:43,033
- I hope what you're doing
isn't wrong.

1833
01:18:43,133 --> 01:18:44,667
- It's all in good cause.

1834
01:18:44,767 --> 01:18:47,133
- Well, I suppose it is.

1835
01:18:47,233 --> 01:18:49,367
Well, if you need me,
you'll find me in my study.

1836
01:18:49,467 --> 01:18:51,433
- Thank you.
You've been grand, Reverend.

1837
01:18:51,533 --> 01:18:53,200
CROW:
And really creepy.

1838
01:18:53,300 --> 01:18:54,333
- What do I do now?

1839
01:18:54,433 --> 01:18:55,333
- Just march down the aisle

1840
01:18:55,433 --> 01:18:57,333
and don't forget to faint
at the altar.

1841
01:18:57,433 --> 01:19:00,233
- Well, here goes nothing.

1842
01:19:00,333 --> 01:19:02,733
TOM:
And the rest of the film.

1843
01:19:02,833 --> 01:19:04,867
- How's everything going?
- Alright so far, I guess.

1844
01:19:04,967 --> 01:19:07,333
- Good.
- Ceremony's about to begin.

1845
01:19:07,433 --> 01:19:08,533
- I certainly hope
somebody shows up

1846
01:19:08,633 --> 01:19:10,567
besides these
ham actors I'm paying.

1847
01:19:10,667 --> 01:19:11,567
- You know, it seems to me

1848
01:19:11,667 --> 01:19:12,700
that if Lorenz
were ready to strike,

1849
01:19:12,800 --> 01:19:13,833
he'd have sent an orchid.

1850
01:19:13,933 --> 01:19:14,933
- Just a minute...

1851
01:19:15,033 --> 01:19:16,133
JOEL:
Hey, it's Lee Harvey Oswald,
you guys.

1852
01:19:16,233 --> 01:19:18,033
- Did you say orchid?
- And I'll take you with it.

1853
01:19:18,133 --> 01:19:20,233
JOEL:
Oh, they nabbed him.

1854
01:19:20,333 --> 01:19:21,300
- There you are.

1855
01:19:21,400 --> 01:19:23,800
TOM:
Well, there's a second actor
theory.

1856
01:19:23,900 --> 01:19:24,833
- It looks like you're right.

1857
01:19:24,933 --> 01:19:26,433
CROW:
No actors in this scene.

1858
01:19:26,533 --> 01:19:27,833
[PIANO NOTES]

1859
01:19:32,400 --> 01:19:35,000
TOM:
They just don't look
conspicuous enough, do they?

1860
01:19:35,100 --> 01:19:36,567
JOEL:
They're the mother and father

1861
01:19:36,667 --> 01:19:37,967
and father of the bride.

1862
01:19:38,067 --> 01:19:39,567
TOM:
Ah.

1863
01:19:39,667 --> 01:19:40,833
CROW [CRIES]:
So sad.

1864
01:19:40,933 --> 01:19:43,600
[TOM AND CROW CRYING]

1865
01:19:43,700 --> 01:19:44,600
JOEL:
Hey, come on.

1866
01:19:44,700 --> 01:19:45,600
It's not a real wedding,
you guys.

1867
01:19:45,700 --> 01:19:46,933
Don't cry.

1868
01:19:47,033 --> 01:19:49,333
CROW:
We know, it's the script
that's upsetting us.

1869
01:19:49,433 --> 01:19:50,433
JOEL:
Mmm-hmm.

1870
01:19:54,800 --> 01:19:56,267
It's the Notre Dame
fight song.

1871
01:19:56,367 --> 01:19:58,333
CROW:
He makes a lovely bride.

1872
01:19:58,433 --> 01:20:00,667
- It looks just like
a real wedding, doesn't it?

1873
01:20:00,767 --> 01:20:03,167
- Yes.
Too bad it isn't...

1874
01:20:04,600 --> 01:20:06,700
ours.

1875
01:20:06,800 --> 01:20:08,533
- Is that a proposal,
Dr. Foster?

1876
01:20:08,633 --> 01:20:09,567
CROW:
It's the best I can do,

1877
01:20:09,667 --> 01:20:11,400
you know I can't gesture.

1878
01:20:11,500 --> 01:20:12,400
JOEL:
He asked me!

1879
01:20:12,500 --> 01:20:14,233
- ...speak to you
in his study, Miss Hunter.

1880
01:20:14,333 --> 01:20:15,467
- But...

1881
01:20:15,567 --> 01:20:16,967
Oh, you mean,
the real Minister.

1882
01:20:17,067 --> 01:20:18,000
- Yes, ma'am.

1883
01:20:18,100 --> 01:20:19,000
- Thank you.

1884
01:20:19,100 --> 01:20:21,100
I'll be right back.

1885
01:20:21,200 --> 01:20:23,667
TOM:
Oh man, did I really
just ask her to marry me?

1886
01:20:23,767 --> 01:20:26,000
What was I thinking?

1887
01:20:29,767 --> 01:20:32,000
- You wanted to see me,
Reverend?

1888
01:20:32,100 --> 01:20:33,667
[TOM GROANS]
[SCREAMS]

1889
01:20:33,767 --> 01:20:34,667
JOEL:
It's the sleeper,

1890
01:20:34,767 --> 01:20:36,333
and that's illegal
submission hold.

1891
01:20:36,433 --> 01:20:38,100
CROW:
Two points.

1892
01:20:38,200 --> 01:20:39,433
- Where's Pat?

1893
01:20:39,533 --> 01:20:41,367
- Minister sent for her.

1894
01:20:45,500 --> 01:20:46,567
JOEL:
Well, what's he gonna do,

1895
01:20:46,667 --> 01:20:49,167
his horticulture, hypnotism,
or ventriloquist act?

1896
01:20:49,267 --> 01:20:50,833
TOM:
Oh, probably ventriloquism.

1897
01:20:50,933 --> 01:20:51,933
Always a crowd pleasure.

1898
01:20:52,033 --> 01:20:54,000
JOEL:
She's in the right position
for it.

1899
01:20:54,100 --> 01:20:56,567
CROW:
Say hello to the folks, Bobby.

1900
01:20:56,667 --> 01:20:59,867
Hello, Bobby, hello.

1901
01:21:03,700 --> 01:21:05,167
- I'll be right back.

1902
01:21:09,000 --> 01:21:09,900
JOEL [IMITATES LUGOSI]:
She'll be perfect

1903
01:21:10,000 --> 01:21:11,767
for my experiments.

1904
01:21:11,867 --> 01:21:13,567
CROW:
She's acting
a little wooden.

1905
01:21:15,500 --> 01:21:16,500
- Oh Pat?

1906
01:21:17,900 --> 01:21:19,767
TOM:
Everyone on stage
for the big Cossack number.

1907
01:21:20,967 --> 01:21:22,733
Five minutes.
JOEL: Hmm?

1908
01:21:22,833 --> 01:21:23,833
CROW:
What the...?

1909
01:21:26,000 --> 01:21:27,467
Cheap door.

1910
01:21:29,833 --> 01:21:31,267
TOM:
A midget in a stairwell.

1911
01:21:31,367 --> 01:21:33,600
You can't really
get much shorter than that.

1912
01:21:38,067 --> 01:21:40,233
CROW:
He makes it seem
like we're in an airplane.

1913
01:21:40,333 --> 01:21:42,933
[♪♪♪]

1914
01:21:47,867 --> 01:21:50,833
JOEL:
There's that
Laurel and Hardy music again.

1915
01:21:50,933 --> 01:21:52,400
[GUNSHOT]

1916
01:21:52,500 --> 01:21:53,700
TOM:
Oh1 Nice shot.

1917
01:21:53,800 --> 01:21:55,133
CROW:
Trying to kneecap Bela.

1918
01:21:55,233 --> 01:21:58,033
- Please, Master!
Don't leave me.

1919
01:21:58,133 --> 01:22:00,133
JOEL:
Oh, that's cold.

1920
01:22:01,267 --> 01:22:03,533
Oh great, Tony.
You shot a midget.

1921
01:22:03,633 --> 01:22:06,933
I hope you sleep well tonight.
Real nice.

1922
01:22:08,633 --> 01:22:10,033
CROW:
I didn't know.

1923
01:22:15,867 --> 01:22:17,667
JOEL:
They got a new
garage door opener.

1924
01:22:17,767 --> 01:22:19,767
TOM:
Cloris is the only one left.

1925
01:22:26,500 --> 01:22:28,967
JOEL:
It's the old fashioned
kind of garage door opener.

1926
01:22:30,067 --> 01:22:31,933
TOM: An old woman?
JOEL: Yeah.

1927
01:22:41,100 --> 01:22:42,367
CROW:
Hey, don't bruise the goods.

1928
01:22:43,367 --> 01:22:44,467
- Toby, my son.
Where is...

1929
01:22:44,567 --> 01:22:45,467
TOM:
Table for one?

1930
01:22:45,567 --> 01:22:46,467
- Toby's gone.
He was careless.

1931
01:22:46,567 --> 01:22:47,467
- You shouldn't have left.

1932
01:22:47,567 --> 01:22:49,867
- [indistinct]
Where's the Countess?

1933
01:22:49,967 --> 01:22:51,633
- Waiting, as usual.

1934
01:22:51,733 --> 01:22:52,933
- Hurry up. Let's go.

1935
01:22:53,033 --> 01:22:55,300
CROW:
She's the worst mother
in the world.

1936
01:23:01,233 --> 01:23:02,333
JOEL:
Oh, that's a great lab.

1937
01:23:02,433 --> 01:23:05,400
Nice dirt floor,
real sanitary, you know?

1938
01:23:06,700 --> 01:23:08,700
[GROANING]

1939
01:23:10,467 --> 01:23:11,567
- There was trouble?

1940
01:23:11,667 --> 01:23:13,467
- Yes, but we'll be finished
before anyone finds us.

1941
01:23:13,567 --> 01:23:14,633
- But the brides.

1942
01:23:14,733 --> 01:23:16,033
What will we do without them?

1943
01:23:16,133 --> 01:23:18,233
- We have to leave them here.

1944
01:23:18,333 --> 01:23:20,033
- Which means I will perish!

1945
01:23:20,133 --> 01:23:21,767
- [indistinct]

1946
01:23:21,867 --> 01:23:24,300
In the meantime,
we take this one with us.

1947
01:23:24,400 --> 01:23:26,300
- What are you waiting for?

1948
01:23:26,400 --> 01:23:27,600
JOEL:
He's gotta dress again,

1949
